WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.097
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4484.

2
00:00:01.097 --> 00:00:02.193
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.193 --> 00:00:06.214
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty I'm here with Ms. Lenoria Ambrose in her lovely home.

4
00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:08.407
Is this Lake Charles [Louisiana] technically?

5
00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:09.138
Lake Charles.

6
00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:13.524
And this is Chelsea Arseneault, and it is Friday June the third.

7
00:00:13.524 --> 00:00:17.910
Its 3:07 in the afternoon, and this is her follow up interview.

8
00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.834
Our first interview was I think in January?

9
00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:21.200
<v LENORIA AMBROSE>Right.

10
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.221
<v ARSENEAULT>It was you and I in January on Oral History Day.

11
00:00:25.221 --> 00:00:34.724
And we just want to follow up with the history of Mossville [Louisiana], and the origins, and your family history, and all that kind of stuff.

12
00:00:34.724 --> 00:00:38.745
And I know you have a lot to share about that.

13
00:00:38.745 --> 00:00:41.303
Well where I first wanted to start,

14
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:49.710
because we've been doing a lot of background research and it’s been kind of difficult to figure out the geographic boundaries of Mossville.

15
00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.172
Like where was it?

16
00:00:51.172 --> 00:00:53.000
Where in the map?

17
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.026
<v AMBROSE>Well the geographic, and I always tell her they laugh at me when I say that.

18
00:00:58.026 --> 00:01:08.391
When the . . . Before the school was built right at the railroad tracks when you turn off of Prater Road and you make a right hand turn and you get to that railroad track.

19
00:01:08.391 --> 00:01:09.647
That was Mossville proper.

20
00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:11.532
Started right there and went west.

21
00:01:11.532 --> 00:01:14.987
And there used to be a sign there that said Mossville.

22
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:18.442
And after the years that went by, the school was built.

23
00:01:18.442 --> 00:01:20.641
The sign was still there, but I

24
00:01:20.641 --> 00:01:25.981
<c.p>don't know who removed the sign or who gave the permission but they took the sign down.

25
00:01:25.981 --> 00:01:32.263
And now they kind of consider all of Bel Air [suburb of Mossville] and all down in that area Mossville.

26
00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:35.090
But Mossville proper is from the railroad track west.

27
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.660
That's where they . . . That's Mossville.

28
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How has the community changed within your life time as far as physical appearance is concerned?

29
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.475
<v AMBROSE>Well when I was young, I married at nineteen and moved to Lake Charles, but my family was still there.

30
00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.717
It was a community of family.

31
00:01:51.717 --> 00:02:01.434
We had a few people that moved in when they brought Queensboro in and, which is off of Prater Road, and Bel Air and Lincoln Heights.

32
00:02:01.434 --> 00:02:04.051
New people came in from Lake Charles.

33
00:02:04.051 --> 00:02:05.172
So it grew.

34
00:02:05.172 --> 00:02:08.535
Mossville really, really grew . . . We had suburbs in Mossville.

35
00:02:08.535 --> 00:02:11.525
Which was Queensboro, Lincoln Heights, and Bel Air.

36
00:02:11.525 --> 00:02:14.889
And but before that time it was mostly family.

37
00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:17.131
We had all the churches there.

38
00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:23.111
We had a Baptist Church, we had two Sanctified Churches or churches of God and Christ.

39
00:02:23.111 --> 00:02:24.980
We had a Methodist Church.

40
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.343
And everybody was . . . belonged to one of those churches.

41
00:02:28.343 --> 00:02:33.949
And then the Catholics went to Westlake [Louisiana] or to Sulphur [Louisiana] for their services.

42
00:02:33.949 --> 00:02:35.818
So the community really changed.

43
00:02:35.818 --> 00:02:38.434
Everybody kept their homes up really well.

44
00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:39.929
They helped each other.

45
00:02:39.929 --> 00:02:41.424
They cut their yards.

46
00:02:41.424 --> 00:02:42.919
It was just beautiful.

47
00:02:42.919 --> 00:02:45.162
It was a beautiful little community.

48
00:02:45.162 --> 00:02:51.515
Well over the years it changed, because more and more people came in that wasn't from Mossville.

49
00:02:51.515 --> 00:02:56.000
Didn't have the same ethics and aesthetics that our family had.

50
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.546
I remember when my grandfather . . . We had six acres of land.

51
00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:03.738
And he cut like half of that when I was a little girl.

52
00:03:03.738 --> 00:03:04.705
With push lawnmower.

53
00:03:04.705 --> 00:03:06.962
That was before they came out with

54
00:03:06.962 --> 00:03:08.896
<c.p>the self-propellers and stuff like that.

55
00:03:08.896 --> 00:03:13.087
And he walked that yard and he picked up every rock, every stone,

56
00:03:13.087 --> 00:03:17.923
every . . . anything that wasn't grass he picked it up before he started cutting his grass.

57
00:03:17.923 --> 00:03:23.727
Now days they just hop on the lawnmower or pushing lawnmower without even looking at anything like that.

58
00:03:23.727 --> 00:03:25.984
But everybody's yards were always very pretty.

59
00:03:25.984 --> 00:03:26.628
Very green.

60
00:03:26.628 --> 00:03:28.563
It was just . . . I don't know.

61
00:03:28.563 --> 00:03:30.820
We had a wonderful, beautiful little community.

62
00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.656
And I look at it now, and I'm just saddened by the way it looks.

63
00:03:35.656 --> 00:03:43.716
Not only because there are no houses there, the houses are all gone, but people don't take care of their property like they used to.

64
00:03:43.716 --> 00:03:48.230
You just didn't have boards hanging off, or yards growing up to your ankles.

65
00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.197
It’s just different.

66
00:03:49.197 --> 00:03:50.486
It's a lot different.

67
00:03:50.486 --> 00:03:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would you describe like the physical appearance in the peak of Mossville?

68
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.160
<v AMBROSE>It was beautiful.

69
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.481
It was a quaint little community.

70
00:03:58.481 --> 00:04:06.604
Like I said, wood frame houses and as time grew we had brick homes built there but it was just beautiful.

71
00:04:06.604 --> 00:04:08.925
It . . . The lawns were well kept.

72
00:04:08.925 --> 00:04:11.245
It was just . . . it was beautiful.

73
00:04:11.245 --> 00:04:13.179
To me, because that's home.

74
00:04:13.179 --> 00:04:15.113
And I have a history.

75
00:04:15.113 --> 00:04:16.660
My history is Mossville.

76
00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:24.009
So, now it's just a downer to see it now, the way it is now, versus what it was.

77
00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:27.877
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were the community leaders when you were growing up?

78
00:04:27.877 --> 00:04:34.066
<v AMBROSE>When I was growing up, my mother . . . They used to call my mother the district attorney.

79
00:04:34.066 --> 00:04:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

80
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:47.500
<v AMBROSE>Gloria Jean Towner Harris and any of our boy or kids would get in trouble my mama had an open door to Frank Salter who was the district attorney here in Lake Charles,

81
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.030
and there was a Mr. [Mazalee?] who was the police detective or chief.

82
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.076
I don't know.

83
00:04:53.076 --> 00:04:55.864
I think he worked for the Calcasieu Parish.

84
00:04:55.864 --> 00:04:59.348
And then there was Ham Reed who was the sheriff.

85
00:04:59.348 --> 00:05:03.879
And any time any of our boys or anybody would get in trouble,

86
00:05:03.879 --> 00:05:12.939
my mom just had to make a telephone call and they would be out and sent home, and then she would take care of it later.

87
00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:15.727
And then Jake Rigmaiden was considered the mayor.

88
00:05:15.727 --> 00:05:18.167
He was a business man in Mossville.

89
00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:19.909
He had a little store.

90
00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:20.258
And . . .

91
00:05:20.258 --> 00:05:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his store?

92
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:34.404
<v AMBROSE>His store was . . . It was just a little . . . Do you know the . . . Have told you where Prater Road comes and makes a . . . all the way down and stops right there just before you go to the rec center?

93
00:05:34.404 --> 00:05:38.974
Well his home was there, and I think you probably talked to his daughter.

94
00:05:38.974 --> 00:05:41.912
She has a funeral home here in Lake Charles.

95
00:05:41.912 --> 00:05:42.891
James Funeral Home.

96
00:05:42.891 --> 00:05:43.870
Lola, Lola James.

97
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.155
Don't know if you've talked to her.

98
00:05:46.155 --> 00:05:47.788
Well, that was her father.

99
00:05:47.788 --> 00:05:52.358
And his . . . It was just . . . I don't even know what they call his store.

100
00:05:52.358 --> 00:05:53.990
I guess just Rigmaiden's Store.

101
00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:59.865
It had . . . You could go there and you could buy chips, and candy, and cookies, and cold cuts.

102
00:05:59.865 --> 00:06:01.824
It was just a small store.

103
00:06:01.824 --> 00:06:09.984
And in fact Mossville at one time had . . . And this was . . . I was very young when it closed but it was a drug store there.

104
00:06:09.984 --> 00:06:15.207
I don't. . . there was a post office but it closed before I got . . . I was born.

105
00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:16.839
We had a service station.

106
00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:20.104
Two . . . Three service stations there on the Old Spanish Trail.

107
00:06:20.104 --> 00:06:21.083
All of them

108
00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:22.715
<c.p>on the Old Spanish Trail.

109
00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.000
There were a lot of businesses.

110
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.016
There were a lot of . . . We had two canteens.

111
00:06:28.016 --> 00:06:28.686
Or three.

112
00:06:28.686 --> 00:06:33.712
When I first started . . . When I was born, there was one called The Sweet Dreams.

113
00:06:33.712 --> 00:06:36.728
I don't know if they told you about that?

114
00:06:36.728 --> 00:06:41.754
Well there was one called The Sweet Dreams and that was owned by [Edie?] Bradley.

115
00:06:41.754 --> 00:06:42.759
Edie Moss Bradley.

116
00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:46.110
Then there was one Martill's which was my grandmother's sister.

117
00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:52.141
She had a barber shop in her store and the canteen where you could go and sit down.

118
00:06:52.141 --> 00:06:56.832
And then a jukebox and you could buy sodas and that sort of thing.

119
00:06:56.832 --> 00:06:58.508
And then we had four.

120
00:06:58.508 --> 00:07:02.864
And then the Lula Lyon's opened the one right by the rec center.

121
00:07:02.864 --> 00:07:07.890
And then we had one down by the school that was by . . . had the Freelows.

122
00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:08.560
The Freelows.

123
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.906
So it was right by the school.

124
00:07:10.906 --> 00:07:13.586
So we had like four canteens in Mossville.

125
00:07:13.586 --> 00:07:15.932
And there was a lot of business.

126
00:07:15.932 --> 00:07:22.634
People had our first grocery store, I think might have been Ira Garrett's which was right at the railroad track.

127
00:07:22.634 --> 00:07:26.319
He had a grocery store, which was close to the school.

128
00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:28.330
And like I said, Jake Rigmaiden.

129
00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:32.351
And later on during the . . . as I grew to be an adult,

130
00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:38.047
we had the Ross family who had a . . . It was a Seven Eleven I think it was.

131
00:07:38.047 --> 00:07:41.398
A chain type thing, and they sold alcohol in there.

132
00:07:41.398 --> 00:07:45.419
The other stores didn't sell alcohol, but they sold alcohol in theirs.

133
00:07:45.419 --> 00:07:48.435
And it was just anything you needed . . . Of course,

134
00:07:48.435 --> 00:07:55.136
if you wanted to buy groceries we had to come to Lake Charles or go to Sulphur for your groceries.

135
00:07:55.136 --> 00:08:04.853
But I remember we would come . . . There was an old bridge before they built the I-10 [Interstate Ten] bridge and we would come down around back across the river.

136
00:08:04.853 --> 00:08:11.890
Might be sort of kind of in the spot where the casino is or just before you get to the casino.

137
00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.236
And we would go to Joyce Therioux’s.

138
00:08:14.236 --> 00:08:17.586
Over on . . . Here in Lake Charles over on Railroad Avenue.

139
00:08:17.586 --> 00:08:18.927
Off of Railroad Avenue.

140
00:08:18.927 --> 00:08:21.607
Where we went to the grocery store here.

141
00:08:21.607 --> 00:08:23.953
Or there was a store in Sulphur.

142
00:08:23.953 --> 00:08:24.958
W. T. Burton's.

143
00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:26.969
I'm sure you've heard of that.

144
00:08:26.969 --> 00:08:28.979
Or have somebody’s told you that.

145
00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:29.314
We

146
00:08:29.314 --> 00:08:33.000
<c.p>would go there with our grandparents, to buy groceries there.

147
00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.103
<v ARSENEAULT>We are definitely going to talk more about that.

148
00:08:36.103 --> 00:08:39.897
I was going to ask about the street names of Mossville?

149
00:08:39.897 --> 00:08:47.828
<v AMBROSE>Those came about when I guess when they . . . As time grew and they . . . When they put in the streets down at Bel Air,

150
00:08:47.828 --> 00:08:56.103
and they put in the streets at Queensboro, and they put in street names at Lincoln Heights, and then they came down into Mossville.

151
00:08:56.103 --> 00:09:00.241
And we had so many little lanes that people were living on,

152
00:09:00.241 --> 00:09:05.414
and they just sort of named them after the people that somebody owned that street.

153
00:09:05.414 --> 00:09:07.138
That lived on that street.

154
00:09:07.138 --> 00:09:10.586
Because where my family's property is, it's called Junius Road.

155
00:09:10.586 --> 00:09:11.966
That's toward the west.

156
00:09:11.966 --> 00:09:13.690
Almost the end of Mossville.

157
00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.793
Which a long time ago was called Perkins Town.

158
00:09:16.793 --> 00:09:21.276
That was the . . . A long time ago we had two areas of Mossville.

159
00:09:21.276 --> 00:09:24.034
Saprack which was Evergreen Road and Perkins Town.

160
00:09:24.034 --> 00:09:26.103
Which was around where we live.

161
00:09:26.103 --> 00:09:31.966
Which was the Perkins and the Mosses and that group of people that lived in that area.

162
00:09:31.966 --> 00:09:33.345
Settled in that area.

163
00:09:33.345 --> 00:09:36.793
But then they started naming the streets after certain families.

164
00:09:36.793 --> 00:09:43.690
And I often wonder why they . . . Where our family was on the corner of Junius and the Old Spanish Trail,

165
00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:47.483
but they named it after one of the old, old settlers.

166
00:09:47.483 --> 00:09:49.207
Her name was Lizzie Hines.

167
00:09:49.207 --> 00:09:53.345
And well, she was a Junius so they named her road Junius.

168
00:09:53.345 --> 00:09:58.172
Well my grandfather gave three quarters of the right of way for the road,

169
00:09:58.172 --> 00:10:04.034
and I thought it should have been named after my family, but they named it after her.

170
00:10:04.034 --> 00:10:09.897
So I was good with that too, because she was a cousin so it didn’t really matter.

171
00:10:09.897 --> 00:10:13.000
And she was an old, old lady so . . .

172
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.094
<v ARSENEAULT>It's kind of interesting, a road being named after a woman.

173
00:10:16.094 --> 00:10:17.500
<v AMBROSE>Yes, it's called Junius Road.

174
00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.469
<v ARSENEAULT>I actually drove down that road today.

175
00:10:19.469 --> 00:10:20.312
I passed up . . .

176
00:10:20.312 --> 00:10:21.156
<v AMBROSE>Oh you did?

177
00:10:21.156 --> 00:10:22.000
<v ARSENAULT>I did.

178
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.674
<v AMBROSE>Yes, well our property is on that corner of Junius Road and the Old Spanish Trail.

179
00:10:27.674 --> 00:10:30.512
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you describe the weddings held in Mossville?

180
00:10:30.512 --> 00:10:31.221
<v AMBROSE>Oh Lord.

181
00:10:31.221 --> 00:10:32.285
Family oriented again.

182
00:10:32.285 --> 00:10:34.413
I remember a long time ago,

183
00:10:34.413 --> 00:10:43.279
when I was a little girl they didn't have a . . . Nowadays a wedding is a big blown out thing where you have food, food, food.

184
00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:44.343
Everything is catered.

185
00:10:44.343 --> 00:10:46.116
You have flowers, flowers, flowers.

186
00:10:46.116 --> 00:10:50.727
When I was a little girl . . . When I was a little bitty girl,

187
00:10:50.727 --> 00:10:58.884
they would just take flowers out of the yard, because everybody had beautiful flowers and they would make the arrangements with those flowers.

188
00:10:58.884 --> 00:11:04.203
And they would have cake, and punch, or lemonade, or they would make ice cream.

189
00:11:04.203 --> 00:11:05.267
Homemade ice cream.

190
00:11:05.267 --> 00:11:12.006
And I remember when my sister got married, there was this bakery here in Lake Charles called Sanitary Bakery.

191
00:11:12.006 --> 00:11:14.488
And they made the different color breads.

192
00:11:14.488 --> 00:11:16.262
Any color bread you wanted.

193
00:11:16.262 --> 00:11:18.390
And my mom made finger sandwiches.

194
00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:22.645
They would make chicken salad sandwiches or egg sandwiches or cucumber sandwiches.

195
00:11:22.645 --> 00:11:26.192
And they would make it with this . . . these different color

196
00:11:26.192 --> 00:11:26.547
<c.p>breads.

197
00:11:26.547 --> 00:11:35.767
And when my mom would serve her circle, which was part of our church, whenever it was her turn, she would make these cute little sandwiches.

198
00:11:35.767 --> 00:11:38.250
But she would use the colored bread.

199
00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.151
If she wanted pink and green she would order her bread.

200
00:11:42.151 --> 00:11:44.279
And they don't do that anymore.

201
00:11:44.279 --> 00:11:46.407
And I often wonder what happened.

202
00:11:46.407 --> 00:11:47.471
Why they stopped.

203
00:11:47.471 --> 00:11:49.599
I know the . . . That bakery closed.

204
00:11:49.599 --> 00:11:55.983
That we don't have really a bakery here in town that . . . in Lake Charles anymore that bakes bread.

205
00:11:55.983 --> 00:11:58.465
We get our bread from somewhere else.

206
00:11:58.465 --> 00:12:04.849
But they . . . It was just . . . You know, I hadn't thought about that now that I'm talking about it.

207
00:12:04.849 --> 00:12:11.587
I don't know what happened that we didn't keep a bakery here that could do those types of things,

208
00:12:11.587 --> 00:12:15.134
because whatever color you wanted any color of the rainbow,

209
00:12:15.134 --> 00:12:21.163
you could get that color bread and make these cute little . . . I call them little demitasse sandwiches.

210
00:12:21.163 --> 00:12:24.000
And it was like little tea sandwiches.

211
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.706
And they used to do those kinds of things, but people got away from proper etiquette.

212
00:12:28.706 --> 00:12:30.765
They sort of just started doing stuff.

213
00:12:30.765 --> 00:12:31.647
Whatever was easy.

214
00:12:31.647 --> 00:12:35.471
And they got away from the way we were taught to do things.

215
00:12:35.471 --> 00:12:38.412
We were taught to, you learn etiquette in high school.

216
00:12:38.412 --> 00:12:39.588
You learned your manners.

217
00:12:39.588 --> 00:12:44.588
Whenever you did something you did it properly you didn't just throw it together and say, "Okay.

218
00:12:44.588 --> 00:12:45.471
That looks okay.

219
00:12:45.471 --> 00:12:46.647
That's what we'll have."

220
00:12:46.647 --> 00:12:55.471
But they went out of their way to do things a little bit different to teach us that there was a way of doing things other than just the ordinary.

221
00:12:55.471 --> 00:12:57.824
You could do things out of the ordinary.

222
00:12:57.824 --> 00:12:59.882
And that's the way I came up.

223
00:12:59.882 --> 00:13:02.235
I was reared in a family like that.

224
00:13:02.235 --> 00:13:04.882
They always went that extra mile to do things.

225
00:13:04.882 --> 00:13:06.059
And I loved that.

226
00:13:06.059 --> 00:13:13.412
And they make fun of me sometimes now my family, because I had a tea party for one of the ladies that I worked with.

227
00:13:13.412 --> 00:13:15.471
Her little girl was having a birthday.

228
00:13:15.471 --> 00:13:19.294
And they weren't going to do anything but just have cake and punch,

229
00:13:19.294 --> 00:13:25.765
so I had a tea party for she and her little cousin and had like three little girls . . . four little girls here.

230
00:13:25.765 --> 00:13:30.176
And I had them dress up with hats and fix little demitasse sandwiches for them.

231
00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:33.118
And it was just really sweet and they enjoyed it.

232
00:13:33.118 --> 00:13:34.000
They had a

233
00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:34.588
<c.p>good time.

234
00:13:34.588 --> 00:13:37.824
But people, they take their kids out to these tea rooms.

235
00:13:37.824 --> 00:13:40.765
But they don't want to do the work at home.

236
00:13:40.765 --> 00:13:47.235
And I had everything dressed up with their names tags at their . . . where they were seated and it was just really cute.

237
00:13:47.235 --> 00:13:48.118
They loved it.

238
00:13:48.118 --> 00:13:49.588
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm sure they loved that.

239
00:13:49.588 --> 00:13:50.176
<v AMBROSE>They did.

240
00:13:50.176 --> 00:13:50.765
They did.

241
00:13:50.765 --> 00:13:52.235
They had a good time.

242
00:13:52.235 --> 00:13:54.000
And I enjoyed doing it.

243
00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.915
<v ARSENEAULT>So the weddings in Mossville.

244
00:13:55.915 --> 00:13:57.064
Who was invited?

245
00:13:57.064 --> 00:14:00.511
<v AMBROSE>The whole community, normally, because it was all family.

246
00:14:00.511 --> 00:14:01.277
Some way.

247
00:14:01.277 --> 00:14:02.426
Marriage or otherwise.

248
00:14:02.426 --> 00:14:05.872
And the whole . . . You always had a big crowd.

249
00:14:05.872 --> 00:14:07.404
Whenever you had anything.

250
00:14:07.404 --> 00:14:09.702
Anything for the churches, birthday parties,

251
00:14:09.702 --> 00:14:18.128
because everybody invited . . . the kids would invite their classmates and then if they had sisters and brothers then they'd have their classmates.

252
00:14:18.128 --> 00:14:19.660
You had more grownups.

253
00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:21.957
And we're still like that now.

254
00:14:21.957 --> 00:14:33.830
When we have something and you have more grownups than you have kids because everybody will bring two or three kids but your friends will come and it's a big party.

255
00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.362
And they enjoy that.

256
00:14:35.362 --> 00:14:38.043
So birthdays were a big thing too.

257
00:14:38.043 --> 00:14:42.255
They always have lots of cake and punch and ice cream.

258
00:14:42.255 --> 00:14:44.936
Or hotdogs or hamburgers or they . . . balloons.

259
00:14:44.936 --> 00:14:48.000
They . . . it was a big thing too.

260
00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.584
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the funerals?

261
00:14:49.584 --> 00:14:54.733
<v AMBROSE>The funerals were . . . When I was young I don't really remember . . . The only

262
00:14:54.733 --> 00:15:02.257
<c.p>person I remember when I was a kid growing up was my grandmother died at forty -six years old.

263
00:15:02.257 --> 00:15:04.238
My mother's mother passed away.

264
00:15:04.238 --> 00:15:04.634
Forty-six.

265
00:15:04.634 --> 00:15:06.614
And I remembered her funeral.

266
00:15:06.614 --> 00:15:13.347
And in fact, most of my family is buried in the cemetery behind Mount Zion Baptist Church.

267
00:15:13.347 --> 00:15:15.723
And that was a big thing.

268
00:15:15.723 --> 00:15:17.307
People came from everywhere.

269
00:15:17.307 --> 00:15:20.871
Wherever you had family, they came to the funeral.

270
00:15:20.871 --> 00:15:23.644
Lot of times they won't come nowadays.

271
00:15:23.644 --> 00:15:26.812
But we had . . . It was a big thing.

272
00:15:26.812 --> 00:15:28.000
All the cousins.

273
00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:33.149
It was like a family reunion, because everybody was there for the funeral.

274
00:15:33.149 --> 00:15:40.673
And most of them were . . . When I was growing up, there were two black funeral homes in Lake Charles.

275
00:15:40.673 --> 00:15:41.861
There was Combre’s.

276
00:15:41.861 --> 00:15:43.446
No, there were three.

277
00:15:43.446 --> 00:15:45.822
Combre’s, Gilmore's, and James Funeral Home.

278
00:15:45.822 --> 00:15:48.990
They handled all the majority of the funerals.

279
00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.554
And later on they . . . Another one was added, Fondel's.

280
00:15:52.554 --> 00:15:54.535
But we always used Gilmore's.

281
00:15:54.535 --> 00:15:59.287
And, they would have the viewing of the bodies in the homes.

282
00:15:59.287 --> 00:16:02.059
They didn't have it in the church.

283
00:16:02.059 --> 00:16:07.208
They would have it either in the funeral home or in the home.

284
00:16:07.208 --> 00:16:09.584
And like we were in Mossville,

285
00:16:09.584 --> 00:16:21.069
so a lot of people didn't have transportation to get to Lake Charles so when my grandmother passed they had it in her . . . in our living room at home.

286
00:16:21.069 --> 00:16:28.990
And then they . . . of course they came and took her to the church and had the funeral at the church.

287
00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:36.911
So a lot of the funerals a long time ago were held in the . . . Viewings were held in the homes.

288
00:16:36.911 --> 00:16:38.891
And they would take pictures.

289
00:16:38.891 --> 00:16:42.851
That's another thing I find strange nowadays I'm an adult.

290
00:16:42.851 --> 00:16:44.832
But they would take pictures.

291
00:16:44.832 --> 00:16:48.000
I don't think anybody does that nowadays.

292
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:49.393
<v ARSENEAULT>Of the dead bodies?

293
00:16:49.393 --> 00:16:50.089
<v AMBROSE>Yes, yes.

294
00:16:50.089 --> 00:16:50.438
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

295
00:16:50.438 --> 00:16:50.786
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

296
00:16:50.786 --> 00:16:51.134
Yes.

297
00:16:51.134 --> 00:16:55.661
I don't know where that came from, where it started, but they did.

298
00:16:55.661 --> 00:16:57.054
A long time ago.

299
00:16:57.054 --> 00:17:03.321
Don't do that anymore and I'm glad they don't because I wouldn't want to take one of me.

300
00:17:03.321 --> 00:17:05.411
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any photos of . . .?

301
00:17:05.411 --> 00:17:07.152
<v AMBROSE>Well I'm trying to think.

302
00:17:07.152 --> 00:17:10.634
Of my grandmother but they're in California with my mom.

303
00:17:10.634 --> 00:17:12.723
We don't have the pictures here.

304
00:17:12.723 --> 00:17:22.473
But I remember when she . . . When my mom moved to California in 1970 she took all the family pictures with her and they . . . Those pictures are in California.

305
00:17:22.473 --> 00:17:25.607
Hadn't thought about that in a long time either.

306
00:17:25.607 --> 00:17:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that?

307
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.242
<v AMBROSE>I hadn't thought about that in a long time either.

308
00:17:31.242 --> 00:17:32.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh yes.

309
00:17:32.091 --> 00:17:35.909
When you were growing up who were your neighbors?

310
00:17:35.909 --> 00:17:41.000
<v AMBROSE>We had . . . Gosh, we had the . . . My godmother and godfather, the Davises.

311
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:45.242
Christine and Fred Davis lived right next door to us.

312
00:17:45.242 --> 00:17:48.636
That was on the west side of us.

313
00:17:48.636 --> 00:17:52.455
On the east side of us, the Hardies lived.

314
00:17:52.455 --> 00:17:57.970
Two doors down . . . Door down we had the LaTours which was Raymond LaTour.

315
00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.939
His father lived next door to him.

316
00:18:00.939 --> 00:18:05.182
Across the street we had the [Guillorys?] and the [Goodlys?].

317
00:18:05.182 --> 00:18:08.576
And the Goodlys were all younger than us.

318
00:18:08.576 --> 00:18:12.818
They were a young family that moved into the neighborhood.

319
00:18:12.818 --> 00:18:14.939
Mr. Hardy had no children.

320
00:18:14.939 --> 00:18:17.061
My godmother had no children.

321
00:18:17.061 --> 00:18:17.485
The

322
00:18:17.485 --> 00:18:19.606
<c.p>LaTours had children my age.

323
00:18:19.606 --> 00:18:25.545
Some of them were my classmates, some were young, and had one son older.

324
00:18:25.545 --> 00:18:34.879
And Mr. LaTour, the father of the LaTours, the grandfather I should say, of the LaTours, he had kids that were older.

325
00:18:34.879 --> 00:18:36.152
Older than us.

326
00:18:36.152 --> 00:18:39.970
We had a lot of boys in our neighborhood.

327
00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:41.667
Maybe half and half.

328
00:18:41.667 --> 00:18:46.758
Or maybe a little bit more with boys than we had girls.

329
00:18:46.758 --> 00:18:51.000
And then down from us we had the Vincents.

330
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:55.146
And I don't know if . . . Where Coach Williams Road is in Mossville?

331
00:18:55.146 --> 00:18:59.292
During the time I was growing up, the Chennault Base was here.

332
00:18:59.292 --> 00:19:01.019
Have you heard of that?

333
00:19:01.019 --> 00:19:02.056
Chennault Air Base?

334
00:19:02.056 --> 00:19:05.856
They have a lot of airmen living here in Lake Charles.

335
00:19:05.856 --> 00:19:10.693
And that's how in east Lake Charles it was called Chennault Air Force Base,

336
00:19:10.693 --> 00:19:14.148
and now it's called . . . I think it's still called Chennault,

337
00:19:14.148 --> 00:19:20.713
but it's an airport now where they bring in planes to be worked on [ . . .?], Boeing, that kind of thing.

338
00:19:20.713 --> 00:19:29.005
Well anyway, they had a lot of airmen here at that time and there was a . . . He was . . . I can't remember his first name,

339
00:19:29.005 --> 00:19:31.078
but his last name was [Renard?].

340
00:19:31.078 --> 00:19:32.805
He was a school teacher,

341
00:19:32.805 --> 00:19:42.825
and he had a whole bunch of homes built in there and we had all these people from all over the United States that lived here that would come.

342
00:19:42.825 --> 00:19:46.280
They would rent these homes on that property he had.

343
00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:53.190
I guess he must have had four or five homes, and single homes and duplexes that was on that property.

344
00:19:53.190 --> 00:20:04.591
So there were a lot of people that came during that time from all over the country because they had somebody that was in the military and they came out there to live.

345
00:20:04.591 --> 00:20:07.355
And so we had a lot of people.

346
00:20:07.355 --> 00:20:15.993
And we were . . . I guess at that time we were lucky to be able to visit with people who had lived all over the world.

347
00:20:15.993 --> 00:20:20.139
And I remember the first time I was ever saw Asian furniture.

348
00:20:20.139 --> 00:20:30.504
They had this . . . I call it [Baroque?] now that I'm older but it was just carved and it was just beautiful as . . . The most beautiful furniture I had ever seen.

349
00:20:30.504 --> 00:20:37.759
And we were able to see that because a lot of them had been to Okinawa, Japan and had lived there.

350
00:20:37.759 --> 00:20:39.487
And they were able to

351
00:20:39.487 --> 00:20:41.905
<c.p>bring furniture back home from their travels.

352
00:20:41.905 --> 00:20:50.888
And I remember my sister-in-law when she . . . She was a school teacher at Sam Houston High in Lake Charles and she . . . her husband went to Germany,

353
00:20:50.888 --> 00:20:57.107
and she couldn't wait to get to Germany because she wanted to be able to bring furniture back.

354
00:20:57.107 --> 00:21:01.599
And when she came back she had these huge things called a shrunk.

355
00:21:01.599 --> 00:21:08.854
But what it was it was a big wall unit with glass doors heavily carved, and you could put different things.

356
00:21:08.854 --> 00:21:10.582
It was like an armoire.

357
00:21:10.582 --> 00:21:13.000
But they called it a shrunk.

358
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:13.627
<v ARSENEAULT>A shrunk?

359
00:21:13.627 --> 00:21:14.255
<v AMBROSE>A shrunk.

360
00:21:14.255 --> 00:21:16.450
And she brought that back from Germany.

361
00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:24.919
So I realized then how these people who lived in all these different places . . . And I have a friend now that's a part of my book club.

362
00:21:24.919 --> 00:21:29.624
She . . . Her husband was in the air force and they lived all over the world.

363
00:21:29.624 --> 00:21:34.642
And she has a lot of beautiful things that she bought when they were in Japan.

364
00:21:34.642 --> 00:21:35.583
They lived there.

365
00:21:35.583 --> 00:21:36.838
She and her kids.

366
00:21:36.838 --> 00:21:41.856
And a lot of women would stay home and their husband was . . . would do the travelling.

367
00:21:41.856 --> 00:21:46.561
Then there were so many of them who, which I think is wonderful . . . I mean,

368
00:21:46.561 --> 00:21:54.716
your kids never stay in one place a long period of time, but they learn to meet different people, and they learn to see different things.

369
00:21:54.716 --> 00:21:59.734
And my sister-in-law used to tell me when they were in Germany how close things were.

370
00:21:59.734 --> 00:22:03.185
We think Germany and France and Spain are so far apart.

371
00:22:03.185 --> 00:22:07.889
But they would drive from Germany to France and just like here too, I guess,

372
00:22:07.889 --> 00:22:13.535
I don't know how many miles it was, but it was nothing for them to drive to France.

373
00:22:13.535 --> 00:22:16.358
And then they would drive from France to Spain,

374
00:22:16.358 --> 00:22:22.004
and then they would have these things they called Volksmarches where they would take and they would walk.

375
00:22:22.004 --> 00:22:25.454
Take a whole weekend and they would walk the Black Forest.

376
00:22:25.454 --> 00:22:27.022
And it was just wonderful.

377
00:22:27.022 --> 00:22:28.277
So, I think that

378
00:22:28.277 --> 00:22:34.236
<c.p>it was something in that taking your kids so that they could see different things and experience different things.

379
00:22:34.236 --> 00:22:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where these airmen . . . Were they coming back from World War II?

380
00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:42.452
<v AMBROSE>They were . . . I think it might have been Northern Korean Conflict.

381
00:22:42.452 --> 00:22:45.690
I think World War II was over with.

382
00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:49.333
But they had the Korean Conflict, for those guys.

383
00:22:49.333 --> 00:22:54.190
<v ARSENEAULT>Did any of the elders ever talk about being in the war?

384
00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:55.000
Fighting . . .

385
00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:57.190
<v AMBROSE>We had . . . My uncles were.

386
00:22:57.190 --> 00:23:03.758
I know one uncle my mom's . . . My grandmother's baby brother was in World War II.

387
00:23:03.758 --> 00:23:06.824
My grandfather's brother was World War II.

388
00:23:06.824 --> 00:23:13.830
And then of course my generation, everybody . . . Almost everybody I knew had a stint in Vietnam.

389
00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:20.837
And which most of the guys that came back from Vietnam they wouldn't talk about it.

390
00:23:20.837 --> 00:23:25.654
They just . . . You couldn't even get them to hold a conversation.

391
00:23:25.654 --> 00:23:31.346
And now some friends that I have now I've mentioned it to them.

392
00:23:31.346 --> 00:23:36.601
Why don't you talk about . . . You have . . . and they all have problems.

393
00:23:36.601 --> 00:23:42.294
They all have anger issues and can't sleep even these many years afterwards.

394
00:23:42.294 --> 00:23:45.359
They just have a lot of problems.

395
00:23:45.359 --> 00:23:54.993
And but the guys it seems like they came back from World War II and the Korean Conflict I don't hear them,

396
00:23:54.993 --> 00:23:57.183
they don't talk about it.

397
00:23:57.183 --> 00:24:02.438
And I guess it was the different ways that wars were handled,

398
00:24:02.438 --> 00:24:16.013
the . . . I don't know what to . . . Maybe in Vietnam it was more hand to hand combat where they actually had to kill people with their bare hands rather than bombs dropping.

399
00:24:16.013 --> 00:24:19.078
I don't know what the difference was.

400
00:24:19.078 --> 00:24:21.268
But they . . . I don't know.

401
00:24:21.268 --> 00:24:23.020
I don't really know.

402
00:24:23.020 --> 00:24:23.895
When we

403
00:24:23.895 --> 00:24:30.026
<c.p>were younger we used to like to talk to them about . . . from my uncles.

404
00:24:30.026 --> 00:24:31.778
From World War II.

405
00:24:31.778 --> 00:24:36.157
And they would tell us the things that they did.

406
00:24:36.157 --> 00:24:39.660
And back then it was a segregated army.

407
00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:48.856
So you had a lot of blacks fighting with other blacks and they would tell us some of things they did.

408
00:24:48.856 --> 00:25:00.242
And then but Vietnam of course it was integrated there, so we had family from both World War II, the Korean Conflict, now I guess everything.

409
00:25:00.242 --> 00:25:01.556
Vietnam, Saudi Arabia.

410
00:25:01.556 --> 00:25:02.869
Up until now.

411
00:25:02.869 --> 00:25:07.686
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would they tell you about the war?

412
00:25:07.686 --> 00:25:09.000
Your uncles.

413
00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:25.436
<v AMBROSE>Well they would talk about the . . . I think some of them were on that when they talked about them landing on these beaches and having to get out of these vehicles that would kind of run up the beach and they would get out and have . . . They would talk about that.

414
00:25:25.436 --> 00:25:29.303
And how, because we used to ask them if they were afraid,

415
00:25:29.303 --> 00:25:34.137
and they said you were afraid but you don't really have time to be afraid,

416
00:25:34.137 --> 00:25:41.872
you're just trying to save your life and save the people you're with, and you got a job to do so you do it.

417
00:25:41.872 --> 00:25:48.640
And I know since I've been growing up, I've talked to some of them and I would ask them I said,

418
00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:52.507
"You know . . ." I said, "When you think about it, you’re trained killers."

419
00:25:52.507 --> 00:25:56.697
And they said at the time you don't think of it that way,

420
00:25:56.697 --> 00:26:01.209
but once you get home and start reflecting and you finally you think “yeah”.

421
00:26:01.209 --> 00:26:08.621
And then when you listen to the music you hear the younger guys who are killing their families who are come home angry.

422
00:26:08.621 --> 00:26:13.455
You think about . . . You reflect on what it was like or why they’re like that.

423
00:26:13.455 --> 00:26:15.711
You can see why they're like that.

424
00:26:15.711 --> 00:26:20.867
And they don't understand why the military or the medical people can't see what they've created.

425
00:26:20.867 --> 00:26:23.445
You know, when they send people to war.

426
00:26:23.445 --> 00:26:26.346
Because killing is not a natural thing, to me.

427
00:26:26.346 --> 00:26:32.469
And I think to the . . . my uncles that I talked to it wasn't a natural thing to them either.

428
00:26:32.469 --> 00:26:36.336
<c.p>But, during those times you were drafted so you had no choice.

429
00:26:36.336 --> 00:26:39.237
You go to war or you got to jail.

430
00:26:39.237 --> 00:26:41.171
I have a couple of uncles.

431
00:26:41.171 --> 00:26:43.749
My mom's brothers, they volunteered for the military.

432
00:26:43.749 --> 00:26:49.227
But I don't think during the time they volunteered it wasn't . . . there was no combat going on.

433
00:26:49.227 --> 00:26:55.028
Then of course something happened and they were sent to . . . One of my uncles was sent to Korea.

434
00:26:55.028 --> 00:26:56.962
That was for the Korean Conflict.

435
00:26:56.962 --> 00:27:01.152
But I never talked to him that much about what his experience was.

436
00:27:01.152 --> 00:27:09.531
But some of the older ones I did talk about and then some of the younger ones I talked to who wanted to talk about it.

437
00:27:09.531 --> 00:27:12.431
We talked about it and that's what they said.

438
00:27:12.431 --> 00:27:16.621
They understand what the Vietnamese guys went through because they were trained killers.

439
00:27:16.621 --> 00:27:21.777
Don't even want to think of yourself as that, but it's either kill or be killed.

440
00:27:21.777 --> 00:27:25.000
That's the way they had to look at it.

441
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.280
<v ARSENEAULT>Does your family all live close?

442
00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:30.700
<v AMBROSE>No. Most of my family now, they're in California.

443
00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:34.500
We have a big bunch of our family in California.

444
00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:37.920
They kind of scattered all over the place now.

445
00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:41.340
We still have a bunch in Mossville, of course.

446
00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:44.000
People that live . . . That are still there.

447
00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:47.800
But my mom's and my immediate family are in California.

448
00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:51.220
So I have a lot of cousins, first cousins.

449
00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:56.540
And I have a sister there, and two brothers, and I have one sister,

450
00:27:56.540 --> 00:28:03.760
when my husband passed in 2005 she came home to visit me and to spend some time with me.

451
00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:04.900
Well she stayed.

452
00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:06.800
She's been here since 2006.

453
00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:11.360
And so I would have had two sisters and two brothers there.

454
00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:17.060
But she came home, so now I just have one sister there and two brothers.

455
00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:20.480
I've been trying to get them to come home.

456
00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:22.000
Come back home.

457
00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:25.023
<v ARSENEAULT>Growing up were you surrounded by family?

458
00:28:25.023 --> 00:28:25.887
<v AMBROSE>Oh yes.

459
00:28:25.887 --> 00:28:28.047
Yes, we were in Mossville.

460
00:28:28.047 --> 00:28:30.206
We knew nothing but family.

461
00:28:30.206 --> 00:28:36.685
I was lucky enough to have . . . to live and my grandparent . . . my grandparents and great-grandparents.

462
00:28:36.685 --> 00:28:43.163
So I was able to know my great-grandparents until I was an adult, and married.

463
00:28:43.163 --> 00:28:48.346
My mom's mama, unfortunately, she died at forty-six of a cerebral hemorrhage.

464
00:28:48.346 --> 00:28:56.553
But then I had her mother that I grew up with, which was my great-grandmother, and then my grandfather.

465
00:28:56.553 --> 00:29:05.191
I was grown and married when he passed, but I had his mom and dad that we grew up with.

466
00:29:05.191 --> 00:29:10.805
And then of course, on my father's side I only knew his mother.

467
00:29:10.805 --> 00:29:12.965
I didn't know his father.

468
00:29:12.965 --> 00:29:16.852
His father I think passed before I was born.

469
00:29:16.852 --> 00:29:23.331
But yes, we had aunties and uncles and we were . . . We had two distinct families.

470
00:29:23.331 --> 00:29:30.673
We had the Towner side who were all loving and down to earth and just loved everybody.

471
00:29:30.673 --> 00:29:32.833
They real huggy kissy type.

472
00:29:32.833 --> 00:29:37.584
Then we had the Perkins side who were a different type.

473
00:29:37.584 --> 00:29:42.767
They were very stanch and upright and everything had to be perfect.

474
00:29:42.767 --> 00:29:49.245
You had to be the best little girl, and you just had to be perfect.

475
00:29:49.245 --> 00:29:54.860
And they were the ones who had more money than the Towner side.

476
00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:59.611
But the Towner side was more giving than the Perkins side.

477
00:29:59.611 --> 00:30:03.930
The Perkins side, they held on to things very tightly.

478
00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:08.249
But the Towners they just, “if I have you have”.

479
00:30:08.249 --> 00:30:13.000
So I guess we had the best of both worlds.

480
00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.167
And we had my great-grandmother, my grandfather's mother.

481
00:30:16.167 --> 00:30:19.729
She was a type that, she just knew things.

482
00:30:19.729 --> 00:30:22.500
She read her Bible all the time.

483
00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:23.688
She was educated.

484
00:30:23.688 --> 00:30:30.021
Both my great-grandmothers were educated as far as they could go in school at that time.

485
00:30:30.021 --> 00:30:33.583
But my . . . They both wrote . . . could read and write.

486
00:30:33.583 --> 00:30:36.354
Both great-grandmothers read . . . could read and write.

487
00:30:36.354 --> 00:30:44.667
And on my grandfather's side, the Towners, I had a great-great-grandfather who learned to read and write at seventy-eight years old.

488
00:30:44.667 --> 00:30:47.042
He had something happen to him.

489
00:30:47.042 --> 00:30:55.750
They lived up at Deridder in Sugartown and he bought, during the time when they had the . . . you could go homestead acreage?

490
00:30:55.750 --> 00:31:02.083
He homesteaded some acreage up there and he couldn't read or write so he depended on

491
00:31:02.083 --> 00:31:05.250
<c.p>someone to take care of it for him.

492
00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:09.208
And the man ended up taking the land from him.

493
00:31:09.208 --> 00:31:14.750
And Grandpa, they said that he said he would never let that happen again.

494
00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:19.500
So he went to the school, at that time it was segregated,

495
00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:28.604
but he went to the black school there and he learned to read and write, so he could know what was going on.

496
00:31:28.604 --> 00:31:31.771
He didn't have to depend on somebody else.

497
00:31:31.771 --> 00:31:32.167
So.

498
00:31:32.167 --> 00:31:36.917
And my family, they were big, both sides, were big on education.

499
00:31:36.917 --> 00:31:38.104
Big on education.

500
00:31:38.104 --> 00:31:47.208
We thought that besides being a person of your word, if you shook hands that was just like signing your name in blood.

501
00:31:47.208 --> 00:31:49.188
That's the way they were.

502
00:31:49.188 --> 00:31:57.104
But they also realized that you had to have an education so that you could know what was going on.

503
00:31:57.104 --> 00:32:01.458
You don't have to depend on anybody else to tell you.

504
00:32:01.458 --> 00:32:04.229
Because you can read, you can write.

505
00:32:04.229 --> 00:32:07.000
So they were sticklers on education.

506
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:07.938
<v ARSENEAULT>The post office?

507
00:32:07.938 --> 00:32:12.938
<v AMBROSE>That shut down before I . . . I don't know if it shut down before I was born.

508
00:32:12.938 --> 00:32:15.750
I think it shut down before I was born.

509
00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:21.688
But they had a post office, and if I'm not mistaken, the post office was in the Rigmaiden Store.

510
00:32:21.688 --> 00:32:24.500
And I have something I'm going to give you.

511
00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:27.938
It's the history of Mossville, there’s two different parts to it.

512
00:32:27.938 --> 00:32:35.125
But I'm going to give it to you, and then I don't know if I gave a copy to Calcasieu [Imperial Calcasieu Museum],

513
00:32:35.125 --> 00:32:38.562
but they, and there's two on my church at Mount Zion.

514
00:32:38.562 --> 00:32:41.062
So I'm going to give you those two.

515
00:32:41.062 --> 00:32:48.250
I'm going to rewrite them and probably get it in some type of order and maybe add a little bit more to them.

516
00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:56.062
I'm just going to give you what I have now, and you can just keep it and I don't know what you'll do with it.

517
00:32:56.062 --> 00:32:57.625
<v ARSENEAULT>It'll be useful for our . . .

518
00:32:57.625 --> 00:33:02.625
<v AMBROSE>Okay so somebody'll type it in . . . But I'm going to try to put those two together.

519
00:33:02.625 --> 00:33:03.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes that'd be great.

520
00:33:03.875 --> 00:33:06.375
<v AMBROSE>Because they left some of the ministers off.

521
00:33:06.375 --> 00:33:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

522
00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:08.582
<v AMBROSE>That were at Mount Zion.

523
00:33:08.582 --> 00:33:14.278
But, and the history of Mossville, I think that's somebody probably gave you a copy of that too.

524
00:33:14.278 --> 00:33:15.861
I'll show it to you.

525
00:33:15.861 --> 00:33:19.658
<v ARSENEAULT>All I've really been able to find is Adventures in Old Calcasieu.

526
00:33:19.658 --> 00:33:25.987
It's a book and then in the book, there's a chapter on Mossville and it's just kind of an outline.

527
00:33:25.987 --> 00:33:28.203
<v AMBROSE>Well I'll show you what I have.

528
00:33:28.203 --> 00:33:28.519
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

529
00:33:28.519 --> 00:33:37.063
<v AMBROSE>But and then you can look at it and see if that's what . . . The post office they . . . And I think in one of these that I have,

530
00:33:37.063 --> 00:33:38.329
one of these histories,

531
00:33:38.329 --> 00:33:45.608
it gives you a little bit of a history of the post office or just tells you that the post office was here.

532
00:33:45.608 --> 00:33:46.557
Really was here.

533
00:33:46.557 --> 00:33:49.405
And I think after Westlake grew and Sulphur grew,

534
00:33:49.405 --> 00:33:57.000
then they had no reason to keep the post office in Mossville because we were kind of in between the two of them.

535
00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:00.153
<v ARSENEAULT>So you just used the post office in . . .?

536
00:34:00.153 --> 00:34:06.064
<v AMBROSE>Half of the . . . Half of Mossville is considered Westlake route, and half is considered Sulphur.

537
00:34:06.064 --> 00:34:08.429
So they did it like that.

538
00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:12.370
<v ARSENEAULT>What are some of your earliest childhood memories of Mossville?

539
00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:20.646
<v AMBROSE>My earliest childhood memories . . . Well some of my earliest childhood memories were there was nothing for us to be afraid of.

540
00:34:20.646 --> 00:34:22.617
We didn't lock our house.

541
00:34:22.617 --> 00:34:31.681
Nobody ever stole from anybody, and I think the first time that anything was ever stolen from us is after I was married,

542
00:34:31.681 --> 00:34:36.016
living over here and nobody lived in our home in Mossville.

543
00:34:36.016 --> 00:34:43.110
Well we didn't lock our . . . I'm trying to remember the first time our house was even ever locked.

544
00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:49.810
I was, I guess in high school and we all left and went to San Antonio [Texas].

545
00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:51.780
And we locked the house.

546
00:34:51.780 --> 00:35:00.845
That was the very first time our house had ever been locked, and we would go to Jeanerette, and DeRidder, and all over.

547
00:35:00.845 --> 00:35:02.027
There was nothing.

548
00:35:02.027 --> 00:35:03.997
Nobody ever stole from anybody.

549
00:35:03.997 --> 00:35:05.574
You had no robberies.

550
00:35:05.574 --> 00:35:14.638
We had very little . . . Well, now that I'm learning more about Mossville there was crime in Mossville but it wasn't that petty crime.

551
00:35:14.638 --> 00:35:16.609
We didn't have petty crimes.

552
00:35:16.609 --> 00:35:20.155
And like I said, everybody looked after each other.

553
00:35:20.155 --> 00:35:22.126
Looked after each other's children.

554
00:35:22.126 --> 00:35:28.432
We could . . . I remember one time we had stopped along the road to pick black berries.

555
00:35:28.432 --> 00:35:36.314
We usually rode the bus, but this time we walked home and we were on the highway and somebody called.

556
00:35:36.314 --> 00:35:43.802
Before we got home my mom had known that we had been stopping on the road picking black berries.

557
00:35:43.802 --> 00:35:48.925
It was. . . The news would just carry, like a carrier pigeon or something.

558
00:35:48.925 --> 00:35:51.684
They knew everything that we were doing.

559
00:35:51.684 --> 00:35:55.231
And you couldn't do anything . . . Somebody could talk to

560
00:35:55.231 --> 00:36:00.354
<c.p>you, whip you, send you home and you might catch a whipping again.

561
00:36:00.354 --> 00:36:03.113
Because everybody took care of everybody's children.

562
00:36:03.113 --> 00:36:05.083
Everybody looked after everybody's children.

563
00:36:05.083 --> 00:36:12.965
And I remember in our church we would have fundraisers for our church, and we had things called penny marches.

564
00:36:12.965 --> 00:36:16.118
I very seldom hear of anything like that.

565
00:36:16.118 --> 00:36:20.453
Church out in Sulphur had one and I . . . A pew rally.

566
00:36:20.453 --> 00:36:22.029
We had pew rallies.

567
00:36:22.029 --> 00:36:22.424
Just . . .

568
00:36:22.424 --> 00:36:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

569
00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:29.407
<v AMBROSE>Where everybody would be responsible . . . Certain ladies in the church would be responsible for a pew.

570
00:36:29.407 --> 00:36:31.097
Like a line of seats.

571
00:36:31.097 --> 00:36:41.234
And you had . . . You would invite people to come and sit in your area and then they would give money and whoever had the biggest amount of money would then,

572
00:36:41.234 --> 00:36:44.276
they would be the winner of the pew rally.

573
00:36:44.276 --> 00:36:49.683
And we had things called cake walks which was, it was drawn out on the floor.

574
00:36:49.683 --> 00:36:55.090
And it was sort of like they would put the different cakes in the different squares,

575
00:36:55.090 --> 00:37:04.552
and you would just march around to music and whenever the music stopped whoever was on that square with that type cake well then they would win it.

576
00:37:04.552 --> 00:37:06.241
Sort of like musical chairs.

577
00:37:06.241 --> 00:37:07.593
Kind of sort of.

578
00:37:07.593 --> 00:37:08.607
We had that.

579
00:37:08.607 --> 00:37:11.310
And it was just so many things that . . .

580
00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What's a penny march?

581
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:17.099
<v AMBROSE>The penny march . . . I can't remember exactly how the penny march went.

582
00:37:17.099 --> 00:37:21.199
But it was sort of like that too but you used pennies.

583
00:37:21.199 --> 00:37:23.932
Where it was music and you would march.

584
00:37:23.932 --> 00:37:27.348
And I was telling my aunt that the other day.

585
00:37:27.348 --> 00:37:34.522
I said, "You remember how the penny march went?' She said, "No." She said, "I can't really remember how it went."

586
00:37:34.522 --> 00:37:35.888
But she said, "I

587
00:37:35.888 --> 00:37:38.963
<c.p>knew we had pennies and you would march around."

588
00:37:38.963 --> 00:37:41.696
But I don't remember exactly how it went.

589
00:37:41.696 --> 00:37:44.429
I'm going to have to ask somebody else.

590
00:37:44.429 --> 00:37:48.186
I hope somebody at the church will remember how it went.

591
00:37:48.186 --> 00:37:52.286
But we had things like that, that they used as a fundraisers.

592
00:37:52.286 --> 00:37:56.727
And they had at the school, they had something called the “womenless weddings”.

593
00:37:56.727 --> 00:38:02.193
Where they had all the guys play all the parts of the ladies in the weddings.

594
00:38:02.193 --> 00:38:03.901
And it was always hilarious.

595
00:38:03.901 --> 00:38:05.950
<v ARSENEAULT>So it wasn't a real wedding?

596
00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.000
<v AMBROSE>No, no, no, no, no.

597
00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:08.664
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

598
00:38:08.664 --> 00:38:13.314
<v AMBROSE>No, no, no, no, no. It was like a play kind of sort of.

599
00:38:13.314 --> 00:38:19.957
But the bride was a man, the bridesmaids were men, and they would be all dressed up and all made-up.

600
00:38:19.957 --> 00:38:21.618
We had things like that.

601
00:38:21.618 --> 00:38:23.279
We had . . . I don't know.

602
00:38:23.279 --> 00:38:28.593
It was just so many things that they had back then that they don't have now.

603
00:38:28.593 --> 00:38:30.918
And then we played outside a lot.

604
00:38:30.918 --> 00:38:32.579
Children played outside a lot.

605
00:38:32.579 --> 00:38:33.907
We were always outside.

606
00:38:33.907 --> 00:38:41.214
And you weren't able to . . . They didn't allow you, once you got through with your chores in the house you went outside.

607
00:38:41.214 --> 00:38:44.536
You didn't stay around grownups, and grownups were in discussion.

608
00:38:44.536 --> 00:38:51.179
We didn't . . . Like some of the people now the children know more about the family happenings than the grownups do.

609
00:38:51.179 --> 00:38:53.171
We weren't allowed to do that.

610
00:38:53.171 --> 00:38:55.164
Children stayed in the children's place.

611
00:38:55.164 --> 00:38:58.154
And my sister and I were talking last year.

612
00:38:58.154 --> 00:39:05.129
She was saying . . . And I was telling her that when I went to work at Olin [Olin Mathieson Lake Charles Plant,

613
00:39:05.129 --> 00:39:06.125
later Olin Chemical

614
00:39:06.125 --> 00:39:07.786
<c.p>Corporation] that was in 1966.

615
00:39:07.786 --> 00:39:09.446
First job I'd ever had.

616
00:39:09.446 --> 00:39:12.768
And I always said that we had an idyllic childhood,

617
00:39:12.768 --> 00:39:20.075
but the only thing I felt that was I missed was not having a job and not knowing how to handle money.

618
00:39:20.075 --> 00:39:22.732
Because my grandfather didn't believe in us working.

619
00:39:22.732 --> 00:39:35.686
And I had wished that I would have had a babysitting job or something that I could learn to handle money and not just wait for Mama to give me my money and do what I wanted to do.

620
00:39:35.686 --> 00:39:39.339
I said but other than that we had an idyllic childhood.

621
00:39:39.339 --> 00:39:41.000
And she said, "Yeah."

622
00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:50.740
I said, "Because I didn't realize that we were poor in the sense of not having a bank account where we had a home and my grandfather didn't owe any money on it.

623
00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:52.215
And he . . . We had cars.

624
00:39:52.215 --> 00:39:55.462
But we didn't have . . . He didn't have a huge bank account,

625
00:39:55.462 --> 00:39:59.594
because he was always taking care of us and taking care of his boys.

626
00:39:59.594 --> 00:40:00.479
His boy's needs."

627
00:40:00.479 --> 00:40:01.955
He said, “You know what?”

628
00:40:01.955 --> 00:40:03.135
She said, "We didn't."

629
00:40:03.135 --> 00:40:05.201
She said, "But we weren't really poor."

630
00:40:05.201 --> 00:40:08.153
She said, "Because we got a roof over our head.

631
00:40:08.153 --> 00:40:11.104
We had food to eat as much as we wanted."

632
00:40:11.104 --> 00:40:16.417
My mom had a . . . Down the street from us was a grocery store, she had an account there,

633
00:40:16.417 --> 00:40:25.271
so when she worked if we needed anything we just went down there and put whatever we wanted on her account and then when she got paid she paid it.

634
00:40:25.271 --> 00:40:28.222
And I said . . . And she said, "When you think about."

635
00:40:28.222 --> 00:40:31.764
She said, "We had six acres of land to run around on."

636
00:40:31.764 --> 00:40:34.125
She said, "There were all kinds of animals."

637
00:40:34.125 --> 00:40:36.781
We had horses and since we can remember cow.

638
00:40:36.781 --> 00:40:37.962
We had a cow.

639
00:40:37.962 --> 00:40:44.160
Then of course we had, when my grandmother was alive, we had chickens and always had rabbits and always had animals.

640
00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.521
She said, "And then we had a pond."

641
00:40:46.521 --> 00:40:51.538
She said, "And there . . . If we wanted to swim we could have swam about in our pond."

642
00:40:51.538 --> 00:40:55.080
She said, "People have ponds nowadays, but that was nothing to us.

643
00:40:55.080 --> 00:41:00.983
And we had a little walk out into the pond that we could walk out on a little docking thing."

644
00:41:00.983 --> 00:41:02.163
And I said, "Yeah."

645
00:41:02.163 --> 00:41:06.000
I said, "I guess we were rich in a lot of ways."

646
00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:10.403
<c.p>And we had lots of love, because we were always told how wonderful we were.

647
00:41:10.403 --> 00:41:12.752
We were always told how smart we were.

648
00:41:12.752 --> 00:41:17.449
So we had no reason to think that we weren't the smartest kids in the world.

649
00:41:17.449 --> 00:41:22.440
And we had it on both sides of the family . . . would tell us how wonderful we were.

650
00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:23.614
How smart we were.

651
00:41:23.614 --> 00:41:25.375
We could do anything we wanted.

652
00:41:25.375 --> 00:41:27.137
We could be anything we wanted.

653
00:41:27.137 --> 00:41:31.246
So, we never had that where . . . I never heard . . . My family didn't use profanity.

654
00:41:31.246 --> 00:41:33.889
They might have used it but not around us.

655
00:41:33.889 --> 00:41:37.998
And when I started working at the plant I was with nothing but men,

656
00:41:37.998 --> 00:41:45.338
and I had never heard . . . And I had . . . My mother had four brothers that we grew up with and they never used profanity around us.

657
00:41:45.338 --> 00:41:52.970
And I would go home crying to my mama in the evening when I get off of work and she was saying, "What's wrong with you?"

658
00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:59.429
She said, "Are they being ugly because . . ." I'm the first black to be hired in the office at Olin at that time.

659
00:41:59.429 --> 00:42:05.006
When I was hired in 1966, I was the first black at Olin, and then there was another girl,

660
00:42:05.006 --> 00:42:09.997
she was a Guillory, she was the first black at PPG [PPG Industries, chemical plant, Lake Charles].

661
00:42:09.997 --> 00:42:16.162
And we were hired in the . . . I was hired in July and I think she was hired in the . . . In June.

662
00:42:16.162 --> 00:42:19.097
Because my husband didn't want me to go to work,

663
00:42:19.097 --> 00:42:24.675
so I had to talk him into letting me, and his mom, and talk them into letting me work.

664
00:42:24.675 --> 00:42:27.024
So she was hired in before I was.

665
00:42:27.024 --> 00:42:33.482
And she thought maybe they were being ugly to me or something and I said, "No, they're not being ugly to me."

666
00:42:33.482 --> 00:42:35.537
She said, "Well why are you crying?"

667
00:42:35.537 --> 00:42:40.234
And I told her I said, "Mama they just use such bad words all the time."

668
00:42:40.234 --> 00:42:41.702
She said, "They do what?"

669
00:42:41.702 --> 00:42:43.463
I said, "They use bad words."

670
00:42:43.463 --> 00:42:45.518
She said, "What kind of bad words?"

671
00:42:45.518 --> 00:42:47.573
She said, "Are they speaking to you?"

672
00:42:47.573 --> 00:42:49.922
I said, "No." "Are they talking about you?"

673
00:42:49.922 --> 00:42:53.151
I said, "No." She said, "Well, let Mama tell you something.

674
00:42:53.151 --> 00:42:54.912
You're in the real world now."

675
00:42:54.912 --> 00:42:57.554
She said, "You're over with a bunch of men.

676
00:42:57.554 --> 00:42:59.903
They're used to using that kind of language."

677
00:42:59.903 --> 00:43:02.838
She said, "So it's time for you to grow up.

678
00:43:02.838 --> 00:43:09.884
As long as they're not talking to you or talking about you, you just let it go in one ear and out the other."

679
00:43:09.884 --> 00:43:20.746
<c.p>And I was still very sensitive to it but I eventually . . . And they were constantly . . . because they knew that I would . . . I don't know if it was the look I had on my face or my flinching.

680
00:43:20.746 --> 00:43:24.268
I don't know what it was but they would apologize to me.

681
00:43:24.268 --> 00:43:30.433
And then finally one day one of the guys said, "Look Lenoria, I'm going to apologize right now for all day."

682
00:43:30.433 --> 00:43:33.369
So he said, "Now I'm not . . .I see your face."

683
00:43:33.369 --> 00:43:35.424
He said, "You still bothered by it?"

684
00:43:35.424 --> 00:43:36.305
I said, "Mhmm.

685
00:43:36.305 --> 00:43:37.772
I'm still bothered by it."

686
00:43:37.772 --> 00:43:40.121
He said, "Well you'll get used to it.

687
00:43:40.121 --> 00:43:42.469
You'll get hardened to it after a while."

688
00:43:42.469 --> 00:43:49.221
He said, "Because you’re with a bunch of men, and they don't mean to hurt your feelings or they're not talking to you."

689
00:43:49.221 --> 00:43:51.570
But he said, "It's just a man's thing."

690
00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:58.028
So, and I'd tell my husband I said . . . You know and he said, "You going to have to get used to that."

691
00:43:58.028 --> 00:44:02.725
He said, "Because men . . ." He said, "I didn't used to use a lot of profanity either."

692
00:44:02.725 --> 00:44:06.542
But he said, "I worked on the docks with a bunch of guys."

693
00:44:06.542 --> 00:44:09.771
He said, " And you get . . . You just get in a rhythm."

694
00:44:09.771 --> 00:44:13.000
So, I still get kind of offended by it but . . .

695
00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:20.453
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any issues being the first black . . . Were you the first black person or first black woman?

696
00:44:20.453 --> 00:44:22.807
<v AMBROSE>First black woman in the office.

697
00:44:22.807 --> 00:44:25.552
<v ARSENEAULT>So they had a black man working?

698
00:44:25.552 --> 00:44:29.475
<v AMBROSE>They had black men working as laborers in the field.

699
00:44:29.475 --> 00:44:32.221
But I was the first black woman.

700
00:44:32.221 --> 00:44:38.497
No and I guess when I was growing up in Mossville, we had one white family.

701
00:44:38.497 --> 00:44:43.989
The Bensons that lived in our community right in fact right down from us.

702
00:44:43.989 --> 00:44:45.558
And those boys were

703
00:44:45.558 --> 00:44:47.127
<c.p>friends with my uncles.

704
00:44:47.127 --> 00:45:00.464
So and then of course my mother worked so we were really used to being around whites all our lives and then our family history . . . we were part Indian part white part black so,

705
00:45:00.464 --> 00:45:03.602
and we were always told who we were.

706
00:45:03.602 --> 00:45:08.702
And except for my great-grandmother she never wanted . . . She knew who she was.

707
00:45:08.702 --> 00:45:11.055
She knew she was half white.

708
00:45:11.055 --> 00:45:14.586
Her mom was black and her dad was white.

709
00:45:14.586 --> 00:45:17.724
But she never wanted to talk about it.

710
00:45:17.724 --> 00:45:21.254
But we knew, because she told my . . . her children.

711
00:45:21.254 --> 00:45:24.000
So they in turn told us.

712
00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:28.147
So we didn't think . . . We thought we was as much as anybody else.

713
00:45:28.147 --> 00:45:38.675
And when I . . . The first . . . I think the first time that I ever came in contact with segregation or felt it was when I went to McNeese [State University] other than that because,

714
00:45:38.675 --> 00:45:40.589
when we rode on the bus,

715
00:45:40.589 --> 00:45:48.883
we would ride the bus from Mossville to Lake Charles to do our little shopping at Sears and Roebuck or the Fair Store something like that,

716
00:45:48.883 --> 00:46:05.791
well we knew that when we got on the bus if there were . . . If Ms. Benson was on the bus or some other white got on the bus because we got to go all the way around English Bayou to Lake Charles that we were to sit the seat behind the front seat.

717
00:46:05.791 --> 00:46:09.939
If nobody was on the bus, we could sit anywhere we wanted to.

718
00:46:09.939 --> 00:46:15.681
So a lot of times it would be nobody on the bus but us and the bus driver.

719
00:46:15.681 --> 00:46:17.595
He didn't care where we sat.

720
00:46:17.595 --> 00:46:23.656
But if somebody white got on, well then we knew that we had to . . . where we had to sit.

721
00:46:23.656 --> 00:46:28.123
So that was just something and then like I said the Bensons, my uncle,

722
00:46:28.123 --> 00:46:32.270
they were around us all the time and then some of the kids.

723
00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:37.693
There was our house and then three other houses and there was a big stretch of woods.

724
00:46:37.693 --> 00:46:45.350
Well part of that property belonged to our family but then after that big stretch of woods there was a . . . the white neighborhood started.

725
00:46:45.350 --> 00:46:55.558
So Ms. Benson had a daughter that lived in that area and then my uncles had the family of guys that would be with the Bensons that were friends with my uncles.

726
00:46:55.558 --> 00:46:57.791
So we were used to seeing that.

727
00:46:57.791 --> 00:47:00.025
I mean when I went to McNeese,

728
00:47:00.025 --> 00:47:06.086
I was in a chemistry class and I had to park . . . I was the only black in the class.

729
00:47:06.086 --> 00:47:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what year was this?

730
00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:08.736
<v AMBROSE>1965 . . . sixty-four.

731
00:47:08.736 --> 00:47:09.104
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

732
00:47:09.104 --> 00:47:09.472
<v AMBROSE>1964.

733
00:47:09.472 --> 00:47:14.990
And I had to partner with someone, so I partnered with a girl from DeRidder.

734
00:47:14.990 --> 00:47:17.197
I think she was from DeRidder.

735
00:47:17.197 --> 00:47:19.773
She chose me to be her partner.

736
00:47:19.773 --> 00:47:23.084
And then one guy that was sitting behind us.

737
00:47:23.084 --> 00:47:26.395
He made the comment that . . . And we were talking.

738
00:47:26.395 --> 00:47:28.234
She and I were talking.

739
00:47:28.234 --> 00:47:36.328
And he made the comment that we have to go to school with them but we don't have to associate with them.

740
00:47:36.328 --> 00:47:41.110
And that was the first inkling that I had ever had of hatred.

741
00:47:41.110 --> 00:47:43.686
It just never even crossed my mind.

742
00:47:43.686 --> 00:47:57.298
Because we went everywhere we wanted to go in Mossville and if we went to Sulphur we were always respected and when we came to Lake Charles we had never . . . I had never heard anything like that.

743
00:47:57.298 --> 00:48:03.920
So that was my first inkling of somebody who could hate somebody for the color of their skin.

744
00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:09.070
<v ARSENEAULT>So your childhood do you just . . . Y'all were sheltered from all the hatred and . . .?

745
00:48:09.070 --> 00:48:09.806
<v AMBROSE>Oh yes.

746
00:48:09.806 --> 00:48:18.268
We knew about it because we could read and we saw what was happening in other areas of the world . . . of the country.

747
00:48:18.268 --> 00:48:22.314
But we . . . We were . . . We never had to deal with it.

748
00:48:22.314 --> 00:48:24.154
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you ever scared that . . .?

749
00:48:24.154 --> 00:48:24.890
<v AMBROSE>No. Never.

750
00:48:24.890 --> 00:48:27.097
Because we had dealt with it.

751
00:48:27.097 --> 00:48:31.880
We knew we heard old people talk about early the Klu Klux Klan.

752
00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:34.455
We knew about the Klu Klux Klan.

753
00:48:34.455 --> 00:48:39.973
We knew that if you went to Moss Bluff you had to be very careful.

754
00:48:39.973 --> 00:48:46.963
But our church would take us to a family in Moss Bluff that was like an excursion for us.

755
00:48:46.963 --> 00:48:48.803
They lived in Moss Bluff.

756
00:48:48.803 --> 00:48:57.264
So they would take us to Moss Bluff but nothing ever happened to us, so we didn't have any reason to be afraid.

757
00:48:57.264 --> 00:48:58.000
And . . .

758
00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:05.048
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to be careful because that's where all the white people lived or Klan people lived?

759
00:49:05.048 --> 00:49:08.181
<v AMBROSE>There were blacks that lived in Moss Bluff.

760
00:49:08.181 --> 00:49:08.964
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

761
00:49:08.964 --> 00:49:10.922
<v AMBROSE>But the Klan was there.

762
00:49:10.922 --> 00:49:13.663
That was a stronghold of the Klan.

763
00:49:13.663 --> 00:49:14.446
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

764
00:49:14.446 --> 00:49:20.319
<v AMBROSE>But nothing ever happened to us because we would go there on our church excursions.

765
00:49:20.319 --> 00:49:25.410
That was like going out of town to . . . it was just Moss Bluff.

766
00:49:25.410 --> 00:49:31.283
From Mossville to Moss Bluff just seemed like it was a long ways to us.

767
00:49:31.283 --> 00:49:36.765
So we never . . . I mean like I said we never had a problem there.

768
00:49:36.765 --> 00:49:42.639
But when I got to be an adult and married and moved over here to

769
00:49:42.639 --> 00:49:47.729
<c.p>Lake Charles and my mother-in-law would tell us some of the horrid stories.

770
00:49:47.729 --> 00:49:51.645
She . . . My husband’s mother . . . I have a picture of her.

771
00:49:51.645 --> 00:49:54.386
Her dad was a tug boat operator.

772
00:49:54.386 --> 00:49:56.343
And she would tell us.

773
00:49:56.343 --> 00:49:58.693
That's my husband's mom and dad.

774
00:49:58.693 --> 00:49:59.476
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh wow.

775
00:49:59.476 --> 00:50:01.042
<v AMBROSE>It's kind of dusty.

776
00:50:01.042 --> 00:50:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They look so nice.

777
00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:04.774
<v AMBROSE>It was their fiftieth anniversary.

778
00:50:04.774 --> 00:50:05.839
<v ARSENEAULT>Aww it's beautiful.

779
00:50:05.839 --> 00:50:07.258
I love her dress.

780
00:50:07.258 --> 00:50:10.452
<v AMBROSE>But her dad was white her mom was black.

781
00:50:10.452 --> 00:50:11.516
Her mom was . . .

782
00:50:11.516 --> 00:50:13.290
<v ARSENEAULT>She's so . . . She's very fair.

783
00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:14.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

784
00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:15.029
<v ARSENEAULT>Very light skinned.

785
00:50:15.029 --> 00:50:16.400
<v AMBROSE>Her daddy was white.

786
00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:20.514
And she would tell us the horrid stories about the Klu Klux

787
00:50:20.514 --> 00:50:20.857
<c.p>Klan.

788
00:50:20.857 --> 00:50:22.914
And she would tell us say . . .

789
00:50:22.914 --> 00:50:24.286
<v ARSENEAULT>She's from Lake Charles?

790
00:50:24.286 --> 00:50:26.000
<v AMBROSE>From here in Lake Charles.

791
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:32.514
And she would tell us about how she had brothers and when they were lynching’s up at Moss Bluff,

792
00:50:32.514 --> 00:50:40.743
people from here who had family members lynched, her dad would have to go with them to get the body and bring it back.

793
00:50:40.743 --> 00:50:46.571
And it was only because he was white they were going to allow him to do that.

794
00:50:46.571 --> 00:51:00.629
And she said that . . . A long time ago she said the reason that she respected the Masons and the Eastern Star so much is that a lot of . . . And she said that was not something that was supposed to be known,

795
00:51:00.629 --> 00:51:04.400
but she said, “I'm old I can tell anything I want”.

796
00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:10.914
She said, but a long time ago, she said it was Masons who saved many a black boy life,

797
00:51:10.914 --> 00:51:19.143
because they would put them in caskets as if they were dead and put them on trains and send their bodies . . . send them out.

798
00:51:19.143 --> 00:51:23.257
Out of the city, so they were able to escape that way.

799
00:51:23.257 --> 00:51:26.000
And she said it was just awful.

800
00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:32.203
She said a long time ago, black boys could be accused of things that they didn't do anything.

801
00:51:32.203 --> 00:51:40.818
If you looked at somebody too long or if you didn't hold your head down when you walked or . . . She just said it was awful.

802
00:51:40.818 --> 00:51:48.399
So I learned more about the things that happened, and then in sixty-five, sixty-six when they first started integrating the schools here.

803
00:51:48.399 --> 00:51:55.635
Well then we had . . . there were crosses burned here in Lake Charles, but we never had anything like that in Mossville.

804
00:51:55.635 --> 00:51:56.669
Happened outside Mossville.

805
00:51:56.669 --> 00:52:00.459
That's one of the things that we didn't . . . we weren't around.

806
00:52:00.459 --> 00:52:02.182
We didn't know anything about.

807
00:52:02.182 --> 00:52:05.628
We were . . . I guess you could say we were sheltered.

808
00:52:05.628 --> 00:52:07.696
We were sheltered from all that.

809
00:52:07.696 --> 00:52:10.453
And then my great-grandparents lived on Highway Ninety.

810
00:52:10.453 --> 00:52:11.486
I don't know.

811
00:52:11.486 --> 00:52:14.932
Have you been right down from Walmart on Highway Ninety?

812
00:52:14.932 --> 00:52:16.311
Have you been to

813
00:52:16.311 --> 00:52:17.000
<c.p>Walmart?

814
00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:18.091
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't think so.

815
00:52:18.091 --> 00:52:23.000
<v AMBROSE>Well on your trips over here when you're going down Prater Road you get to the Highway Ninety.

816
00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:23.273
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

817
00:52:23.273 --> 00:52:24.091
<v AMBROSE>Okay when you . . .

818
00:52:24.091 --> 00:52:26.818
<v ARSENEAULT>I always take a left on that . . . on Highway Ninety.

819
00:52:26.818 --> 00:52:28.455
<v AMBROSE>Okay well you take a right

820
00:52:28.455 --> 00:52:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

821
00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:32.643
<v AMBROSE>Well you say you've been down Coach William's Road?

822
00:52:32.643 --> 00:52:33.048
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

823
00:52:33.048 --> 00:52:34.262
<v AMBROSE>To Highway Ninety?

824
00:52:34.262 --> 00:52:35.476
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't remember.

825
00:52:35.476 --> 00:52:37.095
<v AMBROSE>The Walmart is across.

826
00:52:37.095 --> 00:52:39.524
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it over that bridge thing?

827
00:52:39.524 --> 00:52:42.357
Is that a bridge in Highway Ninety?

828
00:52:42.357 --> 00:52:42.762
No?

829
00:52:42.762 --> 00:52:46.000
I might be thinking of something else.

830
00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:53.132
<v AMBROSE>If you went down Coach Williams Road . . . Where were you going when you went down Coach Williams Road?

831
00:52:53.132 --> 00:52:54.321
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't remember.

832
00:52:54.321 --> 00:52:55.906
We were taking pictures.

833
00:52:55.906 --> 00:52:57.094
I don't remember.

834
00:52:57.094 --> 00:53:01.057
<v AMBROSE>Well if you went left you went to Highway Ninety.

835
00:53:01.057 --> 00:53:01.849
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

836
00:53:01.849 --> 00:53:06.208
<v AMBROSE>And right over to your right is where my great-grandparents lived.

837
00:53:06.208 --> 00:53:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

838
00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:13.094
<v AMBROSE>And they had a lot of land over there because Granny, my great-grandmother, homesteaded a hundred and sixty-seven acres.

839
00:53:13.094 --> 00:53:15.019
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that Bayou D'Inde there or . . .?

840
00:53:15.019 --> 00:53:16.623
<v AMBROSE>Bayou D’Inde [pronounces it D-E-N].

841
00:53:16.623 --> 00:53:18.226
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that what that is?

842
00:53:18.226 --> 00:53:20.792
<v AMBROSE>No. Bayou D’Inde is over to the left.

843
00:53:20.792 --> 00:53:23.038
<v ARSENEAULT>Bayou D’Inde [pronounces it correctly as D-E-N]?

844
00:53:23.038 --> 00:53:24.000
Oh okay.

845
00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:29.298
<v AMBROSE>But anyway . . . And her land went from Highway Ninety over to Old Spanish Trail.

846
00:53:29.298 --> 00:53:31.947
And we would walk through the woods.

847
00:53:31.947 --> 00:53:33.461
Thought nothing of it.

848
00:53:33.461 --> 00:53:38.381
And we would see white people, white guys, white boys hunting in there.

849
00:53:38.381 --> 00:53:45.571
And they were on my great-grandmother's land, but it was her land so we didn't think anything about it.

850
00:53:45.571 --> 00:53:50.491
But we were never hurt or said anything ugly to us or anything.

851
00:53:50.491 --> 00:53:55.032
So we just . . . That was another thing, we didn't pay any attention.

852
00:53:55.032 --> 00:54:07.899
And then when we would go to my great-grandfather's and we had Doctor [Postelle?] Perkins was as a pharmacist from Sulphur and my great-grandfather would tell us to come and shake our cousin's hand.

853
00:54:07.899 --> 00:54:09.034
He was white.

854
00:54:09.034 --> 00:54:12.062
So we had no reason to be afraid.

855
00:54:12.062 --> 00:54:15.846
Or we didn't have the sense enough to be afraid.

856
00:54:15.846 --> 00:54:19.252
Because that's the way we were . . . we grew up.

857
00:54:19.252 --> 00:54:24.550
And he would always say whenever we'd see him, whenever he'd be at Grandpa's,

858
00:54:24.550 --> 00:54:29.849
he tell us to come and shake our cousin's hand, because we were related.

859
00:54:29.849 --> 00:54:32.119
We were related to the Mosses.

860
00:54:32.119 --> 00:54:34.768
And I don't . . . Vincent settled in Sulphur.

861
00:54:34.768 --> 00:54:37.796
Up around, I believe they called it Sabine.

862
00:54:37.796 --> 00:54:46.122
I don't know if it was a parish or what it was, but my great-grandmother would talk about a place called Sabine.

863
00:54:46.122 --> 00:54:52.555
<c.p>So, that kept us, I guess ignorant to the fact of what was going on around us.

864
00:54:52.555 --> 00:54:58.232
And there may have been things going on around in this area, in Mossville area,

865
00:54:58.232 --> 00:55:05.044
or Sulphur . . . Now we knew that Vinton was not a good place, but they had blacks living there.

866
00:55:05.044 --> 00:55:07.693
It wasn't a nice place for blacks.

867
00:55:07.693 --> 00:55:14.505
We knew that Vidor, Texas was not a good place if you got something wrong with your car,

868
00:55:14.505 --> 00:55:18.667
stay in your car, just don't go knocking on people's doors.

869
00:55:18.667 --> 00:55:21.317
And we also heard about Hornbeck, Louisiana.

870
00:55:21.317 --> 00:55:29.642
But our bus stop when we were teenagers in high school, we'd go on to play a school up at Shreveport [Louisiana],

871
00:55:29.642 --> 00:55:36.833
and our bus broke down on us at Hornbeck and we . . . We were on the side of the road.

872
00:55:36.833 --> 00:55:42.131
We had to use the bathroom and we saw way up above some lights.

873
00:55:42.131 --> 00:55:44.780
So we went to this lady's home.

874
00:55:44.780 --> 00:55:45.915
This family’s home.

875
00:55:45.915 --> 00:55:50.456
She let us in, let us use the bathroom, offered us breakfast.

876
00:55:50.456 --> 00:55:51.592
That was Hornbeck.

877
00:55:51.592 --> 00:55:59.161
So there may have been some bad people in Hornbeck but the ones that we came in contact with weren't.

878
00:55:59.161 --> 00:56:02.567
So you just . . . And I think about that sometimes.

879
00:56:02.567 --> 00:56:09.000
I say, "Well, until you're confronted with it, you can just be ignorant to the fact."

880
00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:11.529
<v ARSENAULT>Sounds like it was kind of a blessing.

881
00:56:11.529 --> 00:56:12.162
<v AMBROSE>It was.

882
00:56:12.162 --> 00:56:15.007
It was a blessing and it was a curse.

883
00:56:15.007 --> 00:56:15.324
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

884
00:56:15.324 --> 00:56:20.699
<v AMBROSE>But . . . And then, when I got my job at the plant, I wasn't looking for a job.

885
00:56:20.699 --> 00:56:27.022
I was practicing and going to school, and they had told me that I needed to go for an interview,

886
00:56:27.022 --> 00:56:31.132
because I had never worked before and that was part of my schooling.

887
00:56:31.132 --> 00:56:33.346
That I had to have an interview.

888
00:56:33.346 --> 00:56:39.669
So they sent me to the plant and we were planning on going to the . . . Mexico the fourth of July.

889
00:56:39.669 --> 00:56:43.779
<c.p>And they said, "Well you have to go to an interview to Olin."

890
00:56:43.779 --> 00:56:46.309
And I didn’t even know what Olin was.

891
00:56:46.309 --> 00:56:50.419
I knew it was the plant because we pass it all the time,

892
00:56:50.419 --> 00:56:54.555
but never thinking of . . . My husband always said, "Well you not going to work.

893
00:56:54.555 --> 00:56:56.599
My mama didn't work and you can’t work."

894
00:56:56.599 --> 00:56:57.876
So I said, "Well okay.

895
00:56:57.876 --> 00:56:59.153
I’ll just go play interview."

896
00:56:59.153 --> 00:57:02.474
And when I went the guy called me and he said, "Ms. Ambrose."

897
00:57:02.474 --> 00:57:04.007
He said, "This is Mr. O’Brian."

898
00:57:04.007 --> 00:57:05.796
He said, "Do you want to work?"

899
00:57:05.796 --> 00:57:09.372
And I got excited I said, "Well yes, of course I want to work."

900
00:57:09.372 --> 00:57:14.226
He said, "Well we're going to offer you a job and you have to start on July the sixth."

901
00:57:14.226 --> 00:57:15.248
I said, "Oh okay."

902
00:57:15.248 --> 00:57:16.526
So I told my husband.

903
00:57:16.526 --> 00:57:18.825
I said, "We're not going to go to Mexico."

904
00:57:18.825 --> 00:57:22.657
I said, "I'm going to save that money and buy clothes to go to work."

905
00:57:22.657 --> 00:57:25.978
And he said, "Oh no." He said, "We not going to have that."

906
00:57:25.978 --> 00:57:27.766
I said, "Look, we don't have children.

907
00:57:27.766 --> 00:57:30.577
Just you and I." And I said, "I'm home all day."

908
00:57:30.577 --> 00:57:32.876
He said, "Yeah but you're going back to McNeese."

909
00:57:32.876 --> 00:57:36.964
I said, "But I got a job I don't need to go back to McNeese now."

910
00:57:36.964 --> 00:57:41.051
And my mother-in-law, had to talk to her and I told her what was going on.

911
00:57:41.051 --> 00:57:42.328
And she talked to him.

912
00:57:42.328 --> 00:57:44.628
She told him, "You let her go to work.

913
00:57:44.628 --> 00:57:45.905
If she want to work.

914
00:57:45.905 --> 00:57:47.438
You let her go to work.

915
00:57:47.438 --> 00:57:48.460
That's a wonderful job.

916
00:57:48.460 --> 00:57:52.036
How many black kids and black women are getting good jobs in these plants?

917
00:57:52.036 --> 00:57:53.058
You let her go."

918
00:57:53.058 --> 00:57:54.591
So I said . . . I started working.

919
00:57:54.591 --> 00:57:56.124
I made a promise to him.

920
00:57:56.124 --> 00:57:59.701
I said, "If we have our first child I'll quit and I'll be home."

921
00:57:59.701 --> 00:58:02.000
We never had children, so I kept working.

922
00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:04.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Why didn't he want you to work?

923
00:58:04.333 --> 00:58:06.333
<v AMBROSE>He didn't believe in women working.

924
00:58:06.333 --> 00:58:07.667
His mom never worked.

925
00:58:07.667 --> 00:58:09.000
His mom had children.

926
00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:09.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

927
00:58:09.333 --> 00:58:15.000
<v AMBROSE>And back during that time you could either be a school teacher or work in somebody's house.

928
00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:18.000
His mom thought she was too good for that.

929
00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:24.333
And his dad thought she was too good for that so he wasn't going to let her do that.

930
00:58:24.333 --> 00:58:25.667
And she had babies.

931
00:58:25.667 --> 00:58:27.000
She started having children.

932
00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:30.333
So she stayed home and took care of her children.

933
00:58:30.333 --> 00:58:32.000
It was a big difference.

934
00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:38.667
But it was just the two of us and we had no babies, so I said I wanted to work.

935
00:58:38.667 --> 00:58:40.667
And I worked for forty years.

936
00:58:40.667 --> 00:58:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

937
00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:44.000
<v AMBROSE>Forty years, and the plant shut down in 2006.

938
00:58:44.000 --> 00:58:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow that's a long time.

939
00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:46.817
<v AMBROSE>A long time.

940
00:58:46.817 --> 00:58:48.452
The only job I ever had.

941
00:58:48.452 --> 00:58:50.631
And then since then I've worked at McNeese,

942
00:58:50.631 --> 00:58:55.807
and I put in an application for Delgado [University] because I wanted to go to New Orleans so bad.

943
00:58:55.807 --> 00:59:02.073
When the plant, when I retired in 2012 but before in 2006 I wanted to go to Delgado because I love New Orleans.

944
00:59:02.073 --> 00:59:03.163
I don't know why.

945
00:59:03.163 --> 00:59:04.525
I just love the atmosphere.

946
00:59:04.525 --> 00:59:07.794
I love the . . . I don't know what it is about New Orleans.

947
00:59:07.794 --> 00:59:09.156
I just love the flavor.

948
00:59:09.156 --> 00:59:12.970
<v ARSENEAULT>It's the . . . I [talk to them?] for a while when they come down here.

949
00:59:12.970 --> 00:59:15.150
It's the most different place you'll ever be.

950
00:59:15.150 --> 00:59:18.691
<v AMBROSE>But nobody likes it but me and nobody wants to go with me.

951
00:59:18.691 --> 00:59:22.777
So I said I'm going to shut my house down if I get a job.

952
00:59:22.777 --> 00:59:26.047
Shut my house down and I'll work there all week and I'll

953
00:59:26.047 --> 00:59:27.409
<c.p>come home on the weekend.

954
00:59:27.409 --> 00:59:29.043
And I sent my application in.

955
00:59:29.043 --> 00:59:30.678
I didn't hear anything from them.

956
00:59:30.678 --> 00:59:35.037
So I had a friend that was at McNeese she said, "Come and work at McNeese."

957
00:59:35.037 --> 00:59:36.671
I said, "I don't think so."

958
00:59:36.671 --> 00:59:38.033
So she kept bugging me.

959
00:59:38.033 --> 00:59:39.668
I said, "Alright, I'll do it."

960
00:59:39.668 --> 00:59:44.299
So I sent my application in and got an interview, and got hired and I said, "Well."

961
00:59:44.299 --> 00:59:48.930
I came home and I thought about it and I said, "Maybe I don't want this job."

962
00:59:48.930 --> 00:59:50.565
I said, "Because Delgado might call."

963
00:59:50.565 --> 00:59:53.834
And I was talking to somebody that said, "They may not call."

964
00:59:53.834 --> 00:59:57.103
And I said, "Yeah but I really think they're going to call."

965
00:59:57.103 --> 01:00:00.100
I said, "Okay I'm going to go ahead and take it."

966
01:00:00.100 --> 01:00:04.186
And the week that I started working at McNeese, I got a call from Delgado.

967
01:00:04.186 --> 01:00:05.821
But I'd already taken that job.

968
01:00:05.821 --> 01:00:06.910
So I didn't leave.

969
01:00:06.910 --> 01:00:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That always happens.

970
01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:09.797
<v AMBROSE>Yes, for a reason.

971
01:00:09.797 --> 01:00:13.390
And we have to believe that that's why.

972
01:00:13.390 --> 01:00:15.636
It's for a good reason.

973
01:00:15.636 --> 01:00:25.968
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask you going back to your childhood what were some of the greatest joys or sadness’s that you can remember.

974
01:00:25.968 --> 01:00:30.909
<v AMBROSE>The greatest joys I had was just my life in Mossville.

975
01:00:30.909 --> 01:00:33.604
How I was always cared for.

976
01:00:33.604 --> 01:00:35.850
Always taken good care of.

977
01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:38.545
I grew up around my family.

978
01:00:38.545 --> 01:00:39.444
My grandparents.

979
01:00:39.444 --> 01:00:40.791
My great - grandparents.

980
01:00:40.791 --> 01:00:42.588
My aunts and uncles.

981
01:00:42.588 --> 01:00:43.487
My church.

982
01:00:43.487 --> 01:00:46.631
And I guess really I don't know.

983
01:00:46.631 --> 01:00:50.674
I can’t really think of anything bad right now.

984
01:00:50.674 --> 01:00:52.920
Well when my grandmother died.

985
01:00:52.920 --> 01:00:58.310
I guess that was the worst thing that had happened to me.

986
01:00:58.310 --> 01:01:03.701
And then my mother had a twenty-seven year old brother that passed.

987
01:01:03.701 --> 01:01:05.048
He had tonsillitis.

988
01:01:05.048 --> 01:01:11.337
He worked at Firestone and he . . . They gave him a shot, and it caused

989
01:01:11.337 --> 01:01:15.380
<c.p>him to have a heart attack and he died.

990
01:01:15.380 --> 01:01:21.219
But that was the only two deaths that I really remember growing up.

991
01:01:21.219 --> 01:01:27.508
But as far as my life, like I said we had an idyllic life.

992
01:01:27.508 --> 01:01:30.652
I can't . . . I have no sadness or . . .

993
01:01:30.652 --> 01:01:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's great.

994
01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:32.812
<v AMBROSE>Or anything.

995
01:01:32.812 --> 01:01:37.279
I just can't think of anything that happened that was sad.

996
01:01:37.279 --> 01:01:39.310
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were your role models?

997
01:01:39.310 --> 01:01:40.528
<v AMBROSE>My role models?

998
01:01:40.528 --> 01:01:41.340
<v ARSENEAULT>Growing up.

999
01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:43.777
<v AMBROSE>My goodness I have so many.

1000
01:01:43.777 --> 01:01:44.183
Teachers.

1001
01:01:44.183 --> 01:01:46.619
We had the most wonderful teachers.

1002
01:01:46.619 --> 01:01:49.056
And our teachers cared for us.

1003
01:01:49.056 --> 01:01:49.868
They cared.

1004
01:01:49.868 --> 01:01:52.305
They were friends with our families.

1005
01:01:52.305 --> 01:02:04.487
And they took care of us and made sure that we . . . And my mom is my greatest hero because she gave us everything that we needed to achieve our greatest.

1006
01:02:04.487 --> 01:02:08.142
It was nothing that we couldn’t do in school.

1007
01:02:08.142 --> 01:02:10.985
There was nothing we couldn't participate in.

1008
01:02:10.985 --> 01:02:14.640
She made sure that we had what we needed.

1009
01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:19.919
And I just can't think of . . . And my mother was a single mother.

1010
01:02:19.919 --> 01:02:26.822
But we were reared in the home with my grandfather, and then my uncles, my mother's brothers.

1011
01:02:26.822 --> 01:02:28.853
But she's my greatest hero.

1012
01:02:28.853 --> 01:02:35.756
But other than her and my grandfather, of course the people in my family, my school teachers.

1013
01:02:35.756 --> 01:02:38.599
I had the most wonderful school teachers.

1014
01:02:38.599 --> 01:02:41.036
They were caring, they were giving.

1015
01:02:41.036 --> 01:02:42.254
They would . . . And

1016
01:02:42.254 --> 01:02:47.127
<c.p>during that time that I grew up they didn't spare the rod.

1017
01:02:47.127 --> 01:02:49.563
It didn't kill any of us.

1018
01:02:49.563 --> 01:02:52.000
And that . . . I mean from . . .

1019
01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:54.328
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean you got spanked?

1020
01:02:54.328 --> 01:02:54.793
<v AMBROSE>Right.

1021
01:02:54.793 --> 01:02:56.655
From school to home.

1022
01:02:56.655 --> 01:03:02.241
And in the community, if anybody saw they thought you needed it,

1023
01:03:02.241 --> 01:03:09.224
you better not go home and tell your mama because you'll probably get it again.

1024
01:03:09.224 --> 01:03:10.155
But yes.

1025
01:03:10.155 --> 01:03:10.621
Yes.

1026
01:03:10.621 --> 01:03:14.345
<v ARSENEAULT>Who mainly did the disciplining in the home?

1027
01:03:14.345 --> 01:03:15.276
<v AMBROSE>My mother.

1028
01:03:15.276 --> 01:03:16.207
My mother.

1029
01:03:16.207 --> 01:03:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it just spankings or . . .?

1030
01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:19.582
<v AMBROSE>Oh yes.

1031
01:03:19.582 --> 01:03:25.989
We didn't have . . . The only thing they would . . . punishment was if we didn't go to church we couldn't go to the canteen,

1032
01:03:25.989 --> 01:03:27.445
we couldn't go anywhere else.

1033
01:03:27.445 --> 01:03:28.027
But yes.

1034
01:03:28.027 --> 01:03:34.725
We didn't get that many spankings when we . . . I didn't, when I think about it, because I didn't do anything to get spanked.

1035
01:03:34.725 --> 01:03:42.588
I think the worst thing we got one time was we'd always find something so funny when my mama got on her knees to say her prayers.

1036
01:03:42.588 --> 01:03:43.753
I don't know why.

1037
01:03:43.753 --> 01:03:45.209
We would find funny things.

1038
01:03:45.209 --> 01:03:52.198
And she would thump her foot on the floor to let us know that she could hear us and we needed to quiet down.

1039
01:03:52.198 --> 01:03:56.857
Because she was in her room, we were in our room, but she could hear us.

1040
01:03:56.857 --> 01:04:00.060
And seems like that just made it all the more funny.

1041
01:04:00.060 --> 01:04:01.516
And I think that was

1042
01:04:01.516 --> 01:04:03.555
<c.p>probably the worst whipping I ever caught.

1043
01:04:03.555 --> 01:04:08.214
Was making noise when she was saying her prayers, because we were supposed to be quiet.

1044
01:04:08.214 --> 01:04:08.505
So . . .

1045
01:04:08.505 --> 01:04:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So how were you expected to behave in front of adults?

1046
01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:14.456
<v AMBROSE>Quiet, respectful, yes ma'am, no ma'am.

1047
01:04:14.456 --> 01:04:15.684
Stand up straight.

1048
01:04:15.684 --> 01:04:18.549
That's . . . We were brought up that way.

1049
01:04:18.549 --> 01:04:21.414
We were brought up to be respectful.

1050
01:04:21.414 --> 01:04:27.554
We were brought up to look somebody in the eye when they're talking to you.

1051
01:04:27.554 --> 01:04:34.512
And if it's an adult, I don't care if they were your cousin or who it was,

1052
01:04:34.512 --> 01:04:39.424
you would say yes ma'am and no ma'am and Miss and Mister.

1053
01:04:39.424 --> 01:04:43.517
That was the main thing we were to do especially.

1054
01:04:43.517 --> 01:04:48.429
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you know that was how you were supposed to behave?

1055
01:04:48.429 --> 01:04:52.113
<v AMBROSE>Because we were told and we were shown that.

1056
01:04:52.113 --> 01:04:58.252
We were shown that by our older cousins and were shown that by my mama.

1057
01:04:58.252 --> 01:05:00.299
The way she handle people.

1058
01:05:00.299 --> 01:05:03.574
And my mother was a very talkative person.

1059
01:05:03.574 --> 01:05:06.029
She was a very outgoing person.

1060
01:05:06.029 --> 01:05:08.076
She didn't meet a stranger.

1061
01:05:08.076 --> 01:05:12.169
And she taught us that we were to be respectful.

1062
01:05:12.169 --> 01:05:16.262
We were to say yes ma'am to say no ma'am.

1063
01:05:16.262 --> 01:05:20.355
We were to let older people go ahead of us.

1064
01:05:20.355 --> 01:05:23.221
Open the door if they need it.

1065
01:05:23.221 --> 01:05:26.495
We were taught to care for our elderly.

1066
01:05:26.495 --> 01:05:29.360
And . . . I think about the kids nowadays.

1067
01:05:29.360 --> 01:05:33.453
They let a door slam on your in a minute.

1068
01:05:33.453 --> 01:05:38.775
They don't know how to say yes ma'am no ma'am, thank you, please.

1069
01:05:38.775 --> 01:05:41.230
We were taught all those things.

1070
01:05:41.230 --> 01:05:48.598
At least it might seem kind of silly now, but I think if more children were taught that,

1071
01:05:48.598 --> 01:05:57.194
they wouldn't have time to do so many of the little devilish things that they do, because they would know better.

1072
01:05:57.194 --> 01:05:58.831
They would know better.

1073
01:05:58.831 --> 01:06:00.877
But they're not taught that.

1074
01:06:00.877 --> 01:06:03.333
And I mean it's in every

1075
01:06:03.333 --> 01:06:10.292
<c.p>family, and I never shall forget my brother-in-law used to say that when his kids grew up,

1076
01:06:10.292 --> 01:06:16.431
he wanted to make sure that they had cars when they got to be teenagers.

1077
01:06:16.431 --> 01:06:18.887
Because he never had a car.

1078
01:06:18.887 --> 01:06:28.711
But he had the use of his mom's car and his dad's car, so I never could understand why that wasn't enough for him.

1079
01:06:28.711 --> 01:06:37.306
By the time all his kids got old enough to drive, they were getting their driver's license and they were driving.

1080
01:06:37.306 --> 01:06:40.172
And I used to think about it.

1081
01:06:40.172 --> 01:06:45.083
I used to tell him all the time I said, "You know."

1082
01:06:45.083 --> 01:06:48.358
I said, "You want your kids to drive."

1083
01:06:48.358 --> 01:06:51.632
But I said, "Your kids are not working.

1084
01:06:51.632 --> 01:06:53.679
They don't need to drive.

1085
01:06:53.679 --> 01:06:55.725
They don't need a car."

1086
01:06:55.725 --> 01:06:57.363
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were they going?

1087
01:06:57.363 --> 01:06:59.000
Just all over?

1088
01:06:59.000 --> 01:06:59.346
<v AMBROSE>School.

1089
01:06:59.346 --> 01:07:00.037
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1090
01:07:00.037 --> 01:07:00.728
<v AMBROSE>School activities.

1091
01:07:00.728 --> 01:07:02.802
But they could have taken them.

1092
01:07:02.802 --> 01:07:05.914
And I said, "You don't think about your insurance?"

1093
01:07:05.914 --> 01:07:08.679
And he had one girl and three boys.

1094
01:07:08.679 --> 01:07:10.753
So his insurance was at astronomical.

1095
01:07:10.753 --> 01:07:12.481
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, because boys are more.

1096
01:07:12.481 --> 01:07:12.827
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1097
01:07:12.827 --> 01:07:18.358
And I'm just going to sit back and I'd listen to that and I told Al.

1098
01:07:18.358 --> 01:07:20.778
I said, "Al if we had children."

1099
01:07:20.778 --> 01:07:29.074
I said, "I don't think our children would drive until they were ready to go to college, because we'd be hauling them around everywhere."

1100
01:07:29.074 --> 01:07:32.185
And then the more I think about it, I

1101
01:07:32.185 --> 01:07:37.716
<c.p>say well when we had businesses, so we had to be a little bit more careful.

1102
01:07:37.716 --> 01:07:42.901
We couldn't let just anybody drive our cars, because if they got in a wreck,

1103
01:07:42.901 --> 01:07:49.469
then it would be on us and then somebody knew you had a business, they're wanting to sue you.

1104
01:07:49.469 --> 01:07:51.198
Because he always was self-employed.

1105
01:07:51.198 --> 01:07:53.272
But I worked at the plant.

1106
01:07:53.272 --> 01:07:57.420
And we just had to be very careful of things like that.

1107
01:07:57.420 --> 01:07:59.840
But . . . And I see it even nowadays.

1108
01:07:59.840 --> 01:08:01.568
I have a little cousin.

1109
01:08:01.568 --> 01:08:02.951
She's a smart kid.

1110
01:08:02.951 --> 01:08:05.025
She's a junior in high school.

1111
01:08:05.025 --> 01:08:07.790
I think she'll be a senior next year.

1112
01:08:07.790 --> 01:08:09.173
She's a good athlete.

1113
01:08:09.173 --> 01:08:12.630
She goes right down here to St. Louis High School.

1114
01:08:12.630 --> 01:08:13.321
Catholic school.

1115
01:08:13.321 --> 01:08:17.815
But they just went and bought her a Z . . . whatever that thing is.

1116
01:08:17.815 --> 01:08:18.852
I don't know.

1117
01:08:18.852 --> 01:08:19.889
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a BMW?

1118
01:08:19.889 --> 01:08:21.272
<v AMBROSE>No. A Z something.

1119
01:08:21.272 --> 01:08:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh I don't know.

1120
01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:23.667
<v AMBROSE>Z 280.

1121
01:08:23.667 --> 01:08:25.000
Is that a Nissan?

1122
01:08:25.000 --> 01:08:25.667
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't . . .

1123
01:08:25.667 --> 01:08:28.667
<v AMBROSE>It's whatever that big sports car that they have.

1124
01:08:28.667 --> 01:08:29.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1125
01:08:29.333 --> 01:08:31.333
<v AMBROSE>And I said, "[Laurie?] now Laurie.

1126
01:08:31.333 --> 01:08:34.000
The child has just go a driver's license.

1127
01:08:34.000 --> 01:08:39.000
And you going to turn a child loose in a high performance car like that?"

1128
01:08:39.000 --> 01:08:41.333
It just doesn't make sense to me.

1129
01:08:41.333 --> 01:08:44.000
Maybe it's because I don't have any children.

1130
01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:45.000
I don't know.

1131
01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:49.000
Maybe they'd wear me down too, but I don't think so.

1132
01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:52.005
<v ARSENEAULT>I want to know more about the canteens.

1133
01:08:52.005 --> 01:08:56.137
<v AMBROSE>Well the Dew Drop Inn, I remember seeing a building there,

1134
01:08:56.137 --> 01:09:03.649
but I think by the time I got old enough to go to a canteen . . . I was reared very strict.

1135
01:09:03.649 --> 01:09:07.780
We weren't just allowed to just run all over the place.

1136
01:09:07.780 --> 01:09:12.663
And they, by the time I grew up, it wasn't a canteen anymore,

1137
01:09:12.663 --> 01:09:19.049
it was just a little store that had cookies and candies and stuff you'd go and buy.

1138
01:09:19.049 --> 01:09:23.556
But my uncles grew up at the time that it was there,

1139
01:09:23.556 --> 01:09:31.820
and they said that they had a record player in there and those jukeboxes and it was a hot spot for teenagers.

1140
01:09:31.820 --> 01:09:37.078
And then like I said Aunt Martill she had . . . That was my great-grandmother's sister.

1141
01:09:37.078 --> 01:09:47.220
She had . . . We would love to go there because then we could drink Barq's Root Beers and Barq's strawberries and they had a . . . My cousin cut hair.

1142
01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:50.600
He had a barbershop right in the same building.

1143
01:09:50.600 --> 01:09:56.610
And we would go there and we could dance and my uncles taught us to dance.

1144
01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:58.112
My mother's baby brother.

1145
01:09:58.112 --> 01:10:04.122
He would dance with us at home and so we learned to dance and would listen . . .

1146
01:10:04.122 --> 01:10:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of dances?

1147
01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:13.533
<v AMBROSE>Oh back in that time we had something called the slop and the . . . I can't remember . . . The twist.

1148
01:10:13.533 --> 01:10:14.788
The hully gully.

1149
01:10:14.788 --> 01:10:16.880
And he would teach us.

1150
01:10:16.880 --> 01:10:19.391
And he taught us how to

1151
01:10:19.391 --> 01:10:21.065
<c.p>two-step and to swing.

1152
01:10:21.065 --> 01:10:23.995
And my mother was a wonderful dancer.

1153
01:10:23.995 --> 01:10:26.505
So we would watch her dance.

1154
01:10:26.505 --> 01:10:31.109
We'd play the record player or play her . . . play the radio.

1155
01:10:31.109 --> 01:10:34.875
And we would listen to stations out of Tennessee.

1156
01:10:34.875 --> 01:10:40.315
I don't know, all your stations that you listen to are probably local,

1157
01:10:40.315 --> 01:10:46.592
but ours were . . . During that time we had KLK here, which had a black DJ.

1158
01:10:46.592 --> 01:10:50.777
But all of . . . They were off at a certain time.

1159
01:10:50.777 --> 01:11:00.821
But any time we stayed up late at night we'd have to listen to WLAC Seattle, Nashville, Tennessee, because they played R&amp;B and blues.

1160
01:11:00.821 --> 01:11:05.005
And then we would listen to . . . What was his name?

1161
01:11:05.005 --> 01:11:07.516
He was out of New Mexico.

1162
01:11:07.516 --> 01:11:10.027
He was a very popular DJ.

1163
01:11:10.027 --> 01:11:12.957
I can't think of his name now.

1164
01:11:12.957 --> 01:11:18.397
So we listened to the radio a lot when we weren't watching TV.

1165
01:11:18.397 --> 01:11:20.908
Listened to the radio a lot.

1166
01:11:20.908 --> 01:11:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of music?

1167
01:11:23.000 --> 01:11:24.587
<v AMBROSE>We listened to R&amp;B.

1168
01:11:24.587 --> 01:11:30.935
We listened to . . . Back during that time we listened to country western because that was big.

1169
01:11:30.935 --> 01:11:32.522
And all those stations.

1170
01:11:32.522 --> 01:11:35.300
And we listened to country and western.

1171
01:11:35.300 --> 01:11:36.887
We listened to jazz.

1172
01:11:36.887 --> 01:11:43.235
And of course my mother bought a lot of . . . We were . . . When we were growing up,

1173
01:11:43.235 --> 01:11:52.757
we had a record player and she bought a lot of Arthur Price, or Billy Daniels, Tommy Dorsey, which was a big band sound.

1174
01:11:52.757 --> 01:11:56.328
So I grew up on all kinds of music.

1175
01:11:56.328 --> 01:11:59.105
Whatever my mother liked, we listened to.

1176
01:11:59.105 --> 01:12:03.470
And then what we liked, well we heard on the radio.

1177
01:12:03.470 --> 01:12:07.437
And so I learned to appreciate all kinds of music.

1178
01:12:07.437 --> 01:12:10.215
Don't listen to it as much anymore,

1179
01:12:10.215 --> 01:12:20.927
the big band . . . I can take them in a little dose because now we don't have the same type of big bands that we had back then.

1180
01:12:20.927 --> 01:12:27.275
But we listened to a lot of that, because we listened to everything that she had.

1181
01:12:27.275 --> 01:12:35.607
And we'd buy what we need or listen to . . . We'd listen to . . . There were a lot of . . . When I grew up,

1182
01:12:35.607 --> 01:12:41.162
there were a lot of shows on the radio like they have on TV.

1183
01:12:41.162 --> 01:12:43.146
Like Amos and Andy and

1184
01:12:43.146 --> 01:12:47.113
<c.p>The Gildersleeves, but we listened to them on the radio.

1185
01:12:47.113 --> 01:12:50.287
They were like little shows on the radio.

1186
01:12:50.287 --> 01:12:55.445
So we had a lot of that too, when I was growing up.

1187
01:12:55.445 --> 01:12:57.429
And then we would have . . .

1188
01:12:57.429 --> 01:13:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was the radio like in the living room?

1189
01:13:01.000 --> 01:13:05.412
<v AMBROSE>No our radio . . . We had . . . Well I think they had a couple in the bedrooms,

1190
01:13:05.412 --> 01:13:11.294
but we had a big kitchen, and we had a couch in our kitchen, and a big dining room table.

1191
01:13:11.294 --> 01:13:15.412
And then we would sit in there and listen to the radio in there.

1192
01:13:15.412 --> 01:13:17.765
The kitchen in our house was the hub.

1193
01:13:17.765 --> 01:13:19.529
It was the hub of everything.

1194
01:13:19.529 --> 01:13:23.353
We always . . . We had a little den that we had the TV in.

1195
01:13:23.353 --> 01:13:24.824
We had the living room.

1196
01:13:24.824 --> 01:13:29.824
But most of the times we were in the kitchen, because we had extra seating in there.

1197
01:13:29.824 --> 01:13:33.059
Like I said, we had the dining room table in there.

1198
01:13:33.059 --> 01:13:37.471
So everybody would sit around the dining room table and we listened to the radio.

1199
01:13:37.471 --> 01:13:38.941
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds like so much fun.

1200
01:13:38.941 --> 01:13:40.412
<v AMBROSE>We had a wonderful childhood.

1201
01:13:40.412 --> 01:13:42.765
I mean I can't say enough about it.

1202
01:13:42.765 --> 01:13:43.647
It was just . . .

1203
01:13:43.647 --> 01:13:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever go to the movies?

1204
01:13:46.000 --> 01:13:51.509
<v AMBROSE>Yes we went to . . . There was a movie theater in Sulphur called The Fox.

1205
01:13:51.509 --> 01:13:53.083
We would go there.

1206
01:13:53.083 --> 01:13:56.231
We had to sit up in the balcony.

1207
01:13:56.231 --> 01:13:57.412
It was segregated.

1208
01:13:57.412 --> 01:14:01.347
And then there was one in Westlake called The Lake.

1209
01:14:01.347 --> 01:14:04.102
I think it was called The Lake.

1210
01:14:04.102 --> 01:14:06.856
And we would go to that one.

1211
01:14:06.856 --> 01:14:09.611
And we'd sit up in the balcony.

1212
01:14:09.611 --> 01:14:13.940
Then after I married and moved here there was one downtown

1213
01:14:13.940 --> 01:14:16.301
<c.p>where they had four theaters here.

1214
01:14:16.301 --> 01:14:26.532
They had The Paramount downtown, The Lyric, then over on Railroad Avenue they had The Dixie, and here on Enterprise there was one called The Palace.

1215
01:14:26.532 --> 01:14:30.074
And I would go to those after I married.

1216
01:14:30.074 --> 01:14:34.009
But those were the two theaters we would go to.

1217
01:14:34.009 --> 01:14:36.764
It wasn't something that we did often.

1218
01:14:36.764 --> 01:14:41.486
I remember going to see . . . Let me think of what I saw.

1219
01:14:41.486 --> 01:14:42.273
King Kong.

1220
01:14:42.273 --> 01:14:45.815
I remember seeing that at The Lake in Westlake.

1221
01:14:45.815 --> 01:14:47.389
My uncle took us.

1222
01:14:47.389 --> 01:14:56.440
And I had seen Imitation of Life in San Antonio, but for some reason we saw it again at The Fox in Sulphur.

1223
01:14:56.440 --> 01:14:58.407
The old Imitation of Life.

1224
01:14:58.407 --> 01:15:02.736
And I'm trying to think of some of the other movies.

1225
01:15:02.736 --> 01:15:06.671
Those are the only two that I can really remember.

1226
01:15:06.671 --> 01:15:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any from when you were really young?

1227
01:15:11.000 --> 01:15:15.200
<v AMBROSE>There were a lot of western movies.

1228
01:15:15.200 --> 01:15:17.000
Jean Audrey and . . .

1229
01:15:17.000 --> 01:15:19.400
<v ARSENEAULT>John Wayne type stuff?

1230
01:15:19.400 --> 01:15:25.400
<v AMBROSE>No. There weren't John Wayne movies they were Jean Audrey.

1231
01:15:25.400 --> 01:15:27.200
<v ARSENEAULT>He was before?

1232
01:15:27.200 --> 01:15:30.200
<v AMBROSE>He was before John Wayne.

1233
01:15:30.200 --> 01:15:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1234
01:15:32.000 --> 01:15:36.632
<v AMBROSE>And then of course we watch a lot of TV.

1235
01:15:36.632 --> 01:15:38.949
We watched TV a lot.

1236
01:15:38.949 --> 01:15:44.044
And of course we saw a lot of westerns on TV.

1237
01:15:44.044 --> 01:15:48.213
The Lone Ranger and Dale Evens and Roy Rogers.

1238
01:15:48.213 --> 01:15:49.603
<v ARSENEAULT>Any favorite shows?

1239
01:15:49.603 --> 01:15:51.919
<v AMBROSE>Those were our favorite shows.

1240
01:15:51.919 --> 01:15:53.309
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh they were?

1241
01:15:53.309 --> 01:15:54.235
<v AMBROSE>The westerns.

1242
01:15:54.235 --> 01:15:55.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh that's funny.

1243
01:15:55.625 --> 01:16:00.257
<v AMBROSE>And then we . . . There was one called Father Knows Best.

1244
01:16:00.257 --> 01:16:03.963
Highway . . . Oh what was the name of that?

1245
01:16:03.963 --> 01:16:06.743
Highway . . . It was with Broderick Crawford.

1246
01:16:06.743 --> 01:16:09.059
It was called Highway Fifty-Four.

1247
01:16:09.059 --> 01:16:26.199
He . . . It was kind of a . . . He was like a policeman because they didn't have a lot of . . . They had a lot of shooting and driving fast but there wasn't a lot of killing during those times.

1248
01:16:26.199 --> 01:16:32.684
They didn't let all that . . . TV was a lot different than it is now.

1249
01:16:32.684 --> 01:16:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>No blood and guts.

1250
01:16:35.000 --> 01:16:39.784
<v AMBROSE>No. Your imagination have to put all that in there for you.

1251
01:16:39.784 --> 01:16:42.176
You didn't see any of that.

1252
01:16:42.176 --> 01:16:48.156
But and then we had the . . . Trying to think of some of the old ones.

1253
01:16:48.156 --> 01:16:48.953
We had

1254
01:16:48.953 --> 01:16:50.150
<c.p>Amos and Andy.

1255
01:16:50.150 --> 01:16:54.535
We used to listen to Amos and Andy on the radio.

1256
01:16:54.535 --> 01:16:58.123
Didn't know it was white guys playing black voices.

1257
01:16:58.123 --> 01:17:02.110
And then we'd watch it on TV, they were black.

1258
01:17:02.110 --> 01:17:07.292
But when it first came out there were white characters in black face,

1259
01:17:07.292 --> 01:17:17.259
but then by the time we saw it they had black characters. [ . . .?] And now they're wanting to say that Amos and Andy was type casting.

1260
01:17:17.259 --> 01:17:19.252
It was a bad thing.

1261
01:17:19.252 --> 01:17:23.638
I don't know how they come up with all this stuff.

1262
01:17:23.638 --> 01:17:26.827
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh because they were white in black face?

1263
01:17:26.827 --> 01:17:30.814
<v AMBROSE>Yes, but we saw it when it was black face.

1264
01:17:30.814 --> 01:17:33.605
I mean when it was black guys.

1265
01:17:33.605 --> 01:17:34.402
Black characters.

1266
01:17:34.402 --> 01:17:35.997
They weren't black face.

1267
01:17:35.997 --> 01:17:43.173
I saw . . . I've seen some of those since we can go to the internet and pull them up.

1268
01:17:43.173 --> 01:17:45.166
I've seen some of them.

1269
01:17:45.166 --> 01:17:52.342
But we listened to it on the radio and didn't know it was white guys playing the parts.

1270
01:17:52.342 --> 01:17:57.924
We found that out later as the television and all the reading about it.

1271
01:17:57.924 --> 01:17:59.917
But we didn't know that.

1272
01:17:59.917 --> 01:18:01.113
We just listened.

1273
01:18:01.113 --> 01:18:02.309
It was fun.

1274
01:18:02.309 --> 01:18:03.505
We enjoyed it.

1275
01:18:03.505 --> 01:18:11.080
So if that's what they had to do to . . . It didn't hurt any of us, that I can see.

1276
01:18:11.080 --> 01:18:12.674
Because we didn’t know.

1277
01:18:12.674 --> 01:18:19.850
But I think that was the only one that was black or white guys played the black parts.

1278
01:18:19.850 --> 01:18:23.040
And of course there was [ . . .?] Wilson came out.

1279
01:18:23.040 --> 01:18:31.412
Nat King Cole had a show and of course we listened to . . . We had all those shows that we listened to.

1280
01:18:31.412 --> 01:18:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about The Paradise club?

1281
01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:36.292
<v AMBROSE>I was too young.

1282
01:18:36.292 --> 01:18:39.521
I was too young to go there in its heyday.

1283
01:18:39.521 --> 01:18:45.335
And when I got old enough to go there, they weren't having the bands that they had before.

1284
01:18:45.335 --> 01:18:52.763
And then I got married at nineteen, and of course then moved to Lake Charles so I never . . . I went in there one

1285
01:18:52.763 --> 01:18:54.054
<c.p>time in my life.

1286
01:18:54.054 --> 01:18:55.992
And the thing that happened then,

1287
01:18:55.992 --> 01:19:04.066
my uncle had picked us up and was bringing us to Lake Charles with him and he had to stop off to talk to somebody,

1288
01:19:04.066 --> 01:19:07.296
and he asked us if we wanted to go in.

1289
01:19:07.296 --> 01:19:09.556
It was a weeknight wasn't nobody there.

1290
01:19:09.556 --> 01:19:11.817
He said, "Y'all want to go in?”

1291
01:19:11.817 --> 01:19:14.078
He said, "I'll buy y'all a soda.

1292
01:19:14.078 --> 01:19:16.661
You can sit right here by the door."

1293
01:19:16.661 --> 01:19:21.183
So of course we'd never been in there and we wanted to go in.

1294
01:19:21.183 --> 01:19:27.319
And we went and sat down, and oh my God when we got back home somebody called my grandfather.

1295
01:19:27.319 --> 01:19:30.226
Had told my grandfather that we were in there.

1296
01:19:30.226 --> 01:19:32.163
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a bad thing to . . .?

1297
01:19:32.163 --> 01:19:33.455
<v AMBROSE>In a night club?

1298
01:19:33.455 --> 01:19:35.070
Of course to them, yes.

1299
01:19:35.070 --> 01:19:37.008
To us it wasn't but yes.

1300
01:19:37.008 --> 01:19:42.498
To them that was . . . But by the time . . . And they used to have wonderful, wonderful groups there.

1301
01:19:42.498 --> 01:19:45.728
All there Bobby Blue Band, and all your R&amp;B singers.

1302
01:19:45.728 --> 01:19:53.802
But like I said we weren't allowed because we were young and even when I got into high school my grandfather wouldn't have allowed that.

1303
01:19:53.802 --> 01:19:56.385
That just . . . We were reared very, very strict.

1304
01:19:56.385 --> 01:19:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there alcohol there?

1305
01:19:58.000 --> 01:19:58.386
<v AMBROSE>Yes!

1306
01:19:58.386 --> 01:19:59.157
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . . Okay.

1307
01:19:59.157 --> 01:19:59.543
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1308
01:19:59.543 --> 01:19:59.929
Yes.

1309
01:19:59.929 --> 01:20:01.086
Lots of alcohol.

1310
01:20:01.086 --> 01:20:02.243
<v ARSENEAULT>Who went there?

1311
01:20:02.243 --> 01:20:03.401
<v AMBROSE>Everybody, but us.

1312
01:20:03.401 --> 01:20:04.172
Everybody went.

1313
01:20:04.172 --> 01:20:06.487
People from Lake Charles all around.

1314
01:20:06.487 --> 01:20:09.187
They would have these big groups there.

1315
01:20:09.187 --> 01:20:11.116
It was a big thing.

1316
01:20:11.116 --> 01:20:13.431
It was a big to do.

1317
01:20:13.431 --> 01:20:16.131
He made a lot of money there.

1318
01:20:16.131 --> 01:20:23.075
But like I said, by the time I got old enough to go, it wasn't that big anymore.

1319
01:20:23.075 --> 01:20:25.004
It had run its course.

1320
01:20:25.004 --> 01:20:30.019
They were having . . . I think the civic center was there by that time,

1321
01:20:30.019 --> 01:20:37.348
or they would come over to Ball’s Auditorium here in Lake Charles if they were having big groups there.

1322
01:20:37.348 --> 01:20:43.906
And then I married a . . . My husband was the type that . . . He was old in his head.

1323
01:20:43.906 --> 01:20:46.221
He didn't think his wife should . . .

1324
01:20:46.221 --> 01:20:48.536
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean he was old fashioned?

1325
01:20:48.536 --> 01:20:49.693
<v AMBROSE>Yes, very old.

1326
01:20:49.693 --> 01:20:56.637
For a woman . . . For a man, they can skip up and down the road naked, it didn't matter.

1327
01:20:56.637 --> 01:21:00.880
But for a woman, I could go to the civics’ center.

1328
01:21:00.880 --> 01:21:02.809
The civic center was fine.

1329
01:21:02.809 --> 01:21:07.824
Anybody came to the civic center he didn't have a problem with that.

1330
01:21:07.824 --> 01:21:09.367
But a night club?

1331
01:21:09.367 --> 01:21:17.082
If he was with me, every now and then, that's fine but . . . And he didn't . . . I didn't smoke or drink.

1332
01:21:17.082 --> 01:21:19.783
And he smoked but he didn't drink.

1333
01:21:19.783 --> 01:21:25.955
So he just thought that . . . Well he could go out with his friends that was okay.

1334
01:21:25.955 --> 01:21:27.884
But for a woman . . . Perfect.

1335
01:21:27.884 --> 01:21:29.427
Perfect in all ways.

1336
01:21:29.427 --> 01:21:35.985
So I was the perfect wife. [laughs] But I remember how there would be cars for days.

1337
01:21:35.985 --> 01:21:41.000
Oh gosh and we used to just wish that we could go.

1338
01:21:41.000 --> 01:21:42.179
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it close?

1339
01:21:42.179 --> 01:21:43.357
Where was it?

1340
01:21:43.357 --> 01:21:45.714
<v AMBROSE>Yes it was on Prater Road.

1341
01:21:45.714 --> 01:21:46.107
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1342
01:21:46.107 --> 01:21:49.643
<v AMBROSE>About midway between the Highway Ninety and Prater Road.

1343
01:21:49.643 --> 01:21:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The building's not still there?

1344
01:21:52.000 --> 01:21:52.297
<v AMBROSE>No.

1345
01:21:52.297 --> 01:21:52.595
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1346
01:21:52.595 --> 01:21:54.378
<v AMBROSE>The building is not there anymore.

1347
01:21:54.378 --> 01:21:58.541
You probably see those dump trucks on the right hand side in that lot?

1348
01:21:58.541 --> 01:21:59.730
That's where it was.

1349
01:21:59.730 --> 01:22:00.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1350
01:22:00.027 --> 01:22:01.514
<v AMBROSE>It was a big place.

1351
01:22:01.514 --> 01:22:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it two stories?

1352
01:22:03.000 --> 01:22:04.316
<v AMBROSE>That I don't know.

1353
01:22:04.316 --> 01:22:05.632
I don't think so.

1354
01:22:05.632 --> 01:22:10.566
I think we only went in it one time that was right to the door.

1355
01:22:10.566 --> 01:22:10.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1356
01:22:10.895 --> 01:22:14.184
<v AMBROSE>So I didn't even know what the bar looked like.

1357
01:22:14.184 --> 01:22:19.447
I mean we just walked in the door and sat right to the table right there.

1358
01:22:19.447 --> 01:22:25.039
Didn't get a chance to plunder around and see what the auditorium part of it looked like.

1359
01:22:25.039 --> 01:22:28.987
Because I think where we went in was just like a bar.

1360
01:22:28.987 --> 01:22:29.974
You couldn't get . . .

1361
01:22:29.974 --> 01:22:31.618
<v ARSENEAULT>And the auditorium was separate . . .?

1362
01:22:31.618 --> 01:22:33.263
<v AMBROSE>Had to be somewhere else.

1363
01:22:33.263 --> 01:22:36.224
Because we didn't see any big room or anything.

1364
01:22:36.224 --> 01:22:40.500
And then I wasn't the going out . . . I didn't like . . . I didn't drink.

1365
01:22:40.500 --> 01:22:41.487
I didn't smoke.

1366
01:22:41.487 --> 01:22:43.132
I didn't like loud music.

1367
01:22:43.132 --> 01:22:48.066
So I never . . . And I should have gone just to see what it was about,

1368
01:22:48.066 --> 01:22:51.026
before they tore it down but I never did.

1369
01:22:51.026 --> 01:22:53.000
I never had that urge.

1370
01:22:53.000 --> 01:22:58.400
<v ARSNEAULT>We are at an hour and thirty right now and I still have about three pages of questions.

1371
01:22:58.400 --> 01:22:59.600
Do you want to . . .

1372
01:22:59.600 --> 01:22:59.900
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1373
01:22:59.900 --> 01:23:01.100
<v ARSENEAULT>Schedule a follow up?

1374
01:23:01.100 --> 01:23:01.400
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1375
01:23:01.400 --> 01:23:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>For later?

1376
01:23:02.000 --> 01:23:05.000
<v AMBROSE>Or you got something else you got to do?

1377
01:23:05.000 --> 01:23:06.083
<v ARSENEAULT>I have to be . . .

1378
01:23:06.083 --> 01:23:07.167
<v AMBROSE>Yes we can schedule . . .

1379
01:23:07.167 --> 01:23:07.979
<v ARSENEAULT>In Orange Beach.

1380
01:23:07.979 --> 01:23:08.792
<v AMBROSE>Where is that?

1381
01:23:08.792 --> 01:23:09.333
<v ARSENEAULT>In Alabama.

1382
01:23:09.333 --> 01:23:09.604
For . . .

1383
01:23:09.604 --> 01:23:10.958
<v AMBROSE>You got to drive there?

1384
01:23:10.958 --> 01:23:15.021
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm riding with a friend but I meet them in Baton Rouge and we're driving.

1385
01:23:15.021 --> 01:23:16.104
<v AMBROSE>Oh for the fourth?

1386
01:23:16.104 --> 01:23:18.000
Y'all going there for the fourth?

1387
01:23:18.000 --> 01:23:18.358
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1388
01:23:18.358 --> 01:23:21.585
So I can . . . I don't mind coming back and . . .

1389
01:23:21.585 --> 01:23:21.943
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1390
01:23:21.943 --> 01:23:23.019
<v ARSENEAULT>And I think . . .

1391
01:23:23.019 --> 01:23:24.811
<v AMBROSE>Just whenever you want to.

1392
01:23:24.811 --> 01:23:27.321
<v ARSENEAULT>You have a lot more information probably.

1393
01:23:27.321 --> 01:23:27.679
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1394
01:23:27.679 --> 01:23:36.283
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess the last question would be . . . . Do you remember . . . Well that's a really . . . Well I'll just wait because that's a really deep question.

1395
01:23:36.283 --> 01:23:37.000
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1396
01:23:37.000 --> 01:23:38.289
<v ARSENEUALT>Like what you remember about the elders.

1397
01:23:38.289 --> 01:23:39.947
That could probably . . . You could probably talk about that . . .

1398
01:23:39.947 --> 01:23:40.316
<v AMBROSE>About what?

1399
01:23:40.316 --> 01:23:41.974
<v ARSENEAULT>Just the stories the elders used to tell you.

1400
01:23:41.974 --> 01:23:42.526
<v AMBROSE>Oh my God.

1401
01:23:42.526 --> 01:23:42.726
Yes.

1402
01:23:42.711 --> 01:23:42.911
Yes.

1403
01:23:42.895 --> 01:23:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Probably a lot of those.

1404
01:23:44.000 --> 01:23:44.336
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1405
01:23:44.336 --> 01:23:47.697
<v ARSENEAULT>I know somebody told me Evergreen Road is haunted and . . .

1406
01:23:47.697 --> 01:23:53.074
<v AMBROSE>That's what they say and I . . . I'm . . . They tore down the tree now that they said.

1407
01:23:53.074 --> 01:23:54.082
<v ARSENEAULT>The sycamore tree?

1408
01:23:54.082 --> 01:23:54.418
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1409
01:23:54.418 --> 01:23:59.795
I said, "I don't ever remember seeing . . ." But we didn't walk it, we only rode it.

1410
01:23:59.795 --> 01:24:00.467
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1411
01:24:00.467 --> 01:24:01.139
<v AMBROSE>But I . . .

1412
01:24:01.139 --> 01:24:03.828
<v ARSENEAULT>You don't hold much stock in those tales?

1413
01:24:03.828 --> 01:24:07.189
<v AMBROSE>I . . . Those were the best stories that we ever heard.

1414
01:24:07.189 --> 01:24:08.869
<v ARSENEAULT>That's what I . . . I'm curious.

1415
01:24:08.869 --> 01:24:10.213
<v AMBROSE>Those would be . . . Frightened.

1416
01:24:10.213 --> 01:24:11.557
We'd be so scared.

1417
01:24:11.557 --> 01:24:13.910
But we would sit there and listen.

1418
01:24:13.910 --> 01:24:17.270
Oh my God we just thought that that was heaven.

1419
01:24:17.270 --> 01:24:21.303
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I will come back and I'll get those from you then.

1420
01:24:21.303 --> 01:24:21.639
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1421
01:24:21.639 --> 01:24:22.984
We'll just call it.

1422
01:24:22.984 --> 01:24:23.320
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1423
01:24:23.320 --> 01:24:25.000
Thank you very much.

1424
01:24:25.000 --> 01:24:25.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4484.

1425
01:24:25.429 --> 01:24:25.857
